{:task "seon.ctx/a-turns-diff-tells-an-eval-by-its-handle", :plan "With told? deleted (seon.ctx/the-agent-changes-its-task-by-swap), each turn's :ctx diff (seon.context/world) told every path that changed, an eval the agent made among them, whole: its :seon.eval/record (code, response, :fns), which its own message already shows. Measured, an outside agent's one swap! between two commits: 467 chars with told? (the page part alone), 2,504 without it (about 630 tokens, about 2,000 chars of them the eval's record). The ruling \"The render of ctx never repeats what an eval shows\" holds for the diff too, at the producer, world: the agent's own records are told by what reads them wherever one joins or goes, an eval by its handle (result/<its id>, seon.repl/record!) and a turn by its :seon.turn/id; a path under one is told as any path. [:seon.agent/id] moved at an agent's first recorded eval: seon.agent/begin-run! wrote the agent's entity on its branch by its key alone and seon.repl/record-tx added the id; begin-run! writes the id with the key. An outside agent's read (seon.turn/context-block) tells what changed since the read before it, as a model turn does (seon.context/turn-pages by origin; it counted :model turns only, so each read was the page whole). The same swap!: 224 tokens, about 130 the page part and 90 the :ctx (five eval handles, the plan's old and new); the page part drawing the plan twice is the web-ui session's.", :changed #{"docs/prompt.md#you-are-a-seon-agent" "seon.context/world" "docs/agent.md#ctx" "seon.context/request" "seon.context/turn-pages" "seon.agent/begin-run!"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt 28%: ``seon.ctx/build`` walks the pattern once, when the agent's state first holds its db (a spawn, a resume, a clone), and ``seon.ctx/patch`` keeps it from each transaction's datoms, which ``seon.agent/transact!`` records with the db they made. The walk's state (`:ix`) knows where each entity is held, its pattern, and which `[attribute value]` each join watches (a reverse join's ref to it, a lookup's name). A datom touches a held entity (its own attributes, a forward join) or a watched join; the patch rebuilds each touched entity where it is held, shortest path first, as a build would, keeping every join and member nothing touched, and copies the path above it. A rebuilt entity keeps every attribute equal to before as the object it was, and a rebuild that changes nothing is the old node. A move with no transaction (a merge of main, a resume) is patched from the datoms the two values differ by. A change of the pattern, or one that moves where an entity is held (a new run reaching the task, the holder leaving its window), builds the record anew, as a fresh build places it, and shares back every subtree it did not change (the same objects): that patch costs the record, once a run." "docs/render.md#the-models-context-a-cached-prefix-then-the-turn 24%: A model request sends what does not change first, so the provider serves it from its prefix cache, and sends nothing twice." "docs/ctx.md#changing-it-swap 15%: An agent changes its record as it changes any atom: `(swap! ctx assoc :lab.ctx/steps [...])`, `(swap! ctx update :evals dissoc 1753)`, `(swap! ctx assoc-in [:runs id :seon.run/status] :done)`." "docs/README.md#the-program-and-its-loop 11%: The pages on how agents change Seon's own program and how a text's references and renders show it." "docs/parity.md#context-printing-the-page 5%: Each v1 capability for building and printing the model's context, and where it stands here."], :functions [["seon.context/request 14%: The agent's request, a render of its record: agent a (its entity map, :db/id) for the turn basis b on db (opts :db :basis :help, and :help-ref, the record the system prompt renders): the system prompt (system-text), then the session in tx order (`records`, printed once by printed-run, opts :printed the run's earlier requests' prints, answered again as :printed; within the history budget, `kept`): each eval (eval-messages) and message (message-messages), and at each model turn of the session, the page: the first turn's whole, each later one's what changed since the turn before (`world`: the page's parts by key and ctx's diff), each at the commit its turn's request was made on, after the turn's record." [:seon.config.render/history-budget :seon.model/message]] ["seon.render.ui/render-ctx 11%: The render of an agent's ctx, its layout (the declared html render of :seon.render/render-ctx, which an agent overrides on its branch like any render): its ctx, read through the view, as components placed in the render's three lists, each keyed by what it shows (an eid, a var, a key)." [:seon.ctx/ctx :seon.eval/id :seon.eval/record :seon.message/id :seon.render/opts :seon.task/kind :seon.turn/id :var/name]] ["seon.render.ui/eval-item 10%: An eval of the conversation (a component over ctx), as a reference, never a copy (owner: \"the render should be quite different really from the eval data as the focus for the ui is on the prose\"): the prose the agent wrote beside it and its handle (result/e12), which names its value wherever the transcript and a printed cut name it; the model reads its content once, in its transcript." [:seon.agent/id :seon.agent/key :seon.ctx/ctx :seon.eval/agent :seon.eval/id :seon.eval/opened :seon.eval/record :seon.render/opts]] ["seon.turn/act! 7%: One model turn: the context of what was observed to the model, the reply's eval calls through the one REPL path, each answered as its own result, the turn's facts on the branch." [:seon.config.ai/cache-block-tokens :seon.run/id :seon.task/id :seon.turn/basis :seon.turn/calls :seon.turn/error :seon.turn/errors :seon.turn/finish :seon.turn/forms :seon.turn/id :seon.turn/model :seon.turn/ms :seon.turn/n :seon.turn/origin :seon.turn/reasoning :seon.turn/red :seon.turn/reply :seon.turn/run :seon.turn/sent :seon.turn/split :seon.turn/tokens :seon.turn/truncated]] "seon.ctx/heard-keys 6%: What the agent last heard of main and of its branch's merge base (seon.agent's state :main and :base): the record's basis like the environment, never written, but diffed as data, so a render reading them follows main."], :schemas [[:seon.ctx/ctx "19%"] [:seon.eval/record "17%"] [:seon.task/state "5%"] [:seon.render/sent-request "4%"] [:seon.run/run-fact "4%"]]}}
{:task "seon.proofs/prove-is-the-one-runner", :plan "Root's audit (audit-dup-system, item 1, with the cold review's cuts), 2026-10-27: seon.prove admits and records every test run, but two callers bypassed it and the process tests had two filters and a second reading of results. Done: my.agent/prove asks the service (seon.agent/prove! :judge; given proofs, a run of their namespaces, :names now forwarded by seon.agent-proc), so an agent's REPL run is recorded on main and is one run with a turn's of the same head; seon.root/run-tests! asks it too (seon.root/prove! in mode :judge; its ticks arity went, the ROBUST check's tick-bound case evaluates seon.agent/run-tests-forked in the agent); one process filter, seon.proofs/left-out, for bin/test's plan and seon.agent/run-selection (test-runner/automatic deleted); the merge gate's :tests effect reads each test's verdict at the digest prove's plan wrote (out/tests.edn) in the results directory, not prove's printed RED/SHARD lines (fx/test-results' regex deleted); and the process tests a change reaches gate its merge with nobody asking (owner's ruling 2026-10-27; seon.reconcile no longer reads :seon.task/gate?, whose code stays until THE PLAN step 0.7 exercises the gate). The gate's checkout, undeclared, is the root's own source root (seon.root/start!), and a root with neither holds such a merge with its reason. Not done: the dashboard's run-tests! (task seon.proofs/the-dashboards-run-tests-asks-the-service).", :changed #{"seon.prove/request" "seon.prove/prove-proc" "seon.fork-server/server-step" "seon.prove/answered" "seon.root/start!" "seon.fork-server/graph-at-fork" ":seon.task/evidence" "seon.reconcile/done-explain" "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches" "seon.program/moves-program?" "seon.agent/run-tests-forked" "my.root/gate!" "docs/merging.md#the-test-service" "seon.fx/run-tests" "seon.prove/slot" "seon.proofs/left-out" "seon.fx/test-results" "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-