t: seon.proofs/plan of its tests' reach digests (seon.program/reach-digests) against base's (main at its merge base), the greens stored at those digests (files in results-dir, and the test service's result facts in db, greens and reds: seon.proofs/stored-results) and proofs." [:form/src :ns/form :ns/name :seon.config.source/test-results-dir :var/form :var/kind :var/name]] ["seon.agent/handle! 5%: One Seon platform request; answers the messages to send back (a vector)." [:seon.config.agent/test-ticks :seon.wire/seon-msg]] ["seon.contract/on? 4%: Are the contracts on in this process (:seon.config.contract/instrument)? :off: no wrapper and no recording; :contracts: wrappers, no recording transaction (for measuring the two apart); :on: both." [:seon.config.contract/instrument]] "seon.agent/prove! 4%: Ask the test service (seon.prove) to prove this branch at its head, and wait for its answer: the run's (run-tests-forked's answer, with :key)." ["seon.agent/run-tests-here 3%: The tests a test-plan runs (var symbols), by the one runner (seon.proofs/run-tests!, nothing stored: the parent holds the greens), with the branch's schemas in effect (malli's dynamic registry bound to seon.program/schema-registry, as the agent's REPL binds it), each test within ticks engine ticks of work of its own (the runner's bound): answers report data." [:seon.test/report :seon.test/result]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.source/gate-checkout "11%"] [:seon.config.proofs/jobs "5%"] [:seon.task/state "5%"] [:seon.config.source/test-results-dir "5%"] [:seon.config.agent/test-ticks "4%"]]}}
{:task "seon.cost/a-call-allocates-the-same-bytes-each-time", :plan "Bytes are exact (AGENTS 5), yet seon.merge-cost-test/a-three-way-costs-the-changes-not-the-program went red once (n^0.92 against 0.91) and green on an equal tree: the same call on the same input allocated a few hundred bytes more or less (seon.merge/delta-three-way 3,709,584-3,713,248 B at n=300). Found so far, measured: a pure function is exact; each Datahike read alone is near exact; in bare Jolt with this thread's count (Chez thread_bytes_allocated, which our fork already uncounts for a collection and its handler, s/7.ss $call-uncounted) one thread's work is exact through collections (one +128 B in 40), but with a second thread allocating, a stack-deep lazy read (subseq x100 on a sorted set) gains +96 or +192 B on runs a collection lands in, while a flat one (vector build) stays exact; uncounting Chez's collect rendezvous changes nothing. Not a stack split: uncounting all of c/schsig.c's S_split_and_resize and S_overflow's split continuation (S_thread_bytes_uncount around them, as alloc.c uncounts a new stack) left it as it was (subseq x100: +96 B on 7-8 of 40 runs, measured twice on a Chez built with it); reverted. What holds: always +96 B (+192 rarely), only on runs a collection lands in, only with another thread allocating, only in the stack-deep lazy read, never single-threaded. Candidates, untested: (a) THE NEXT STEP: the event path, a thread brought to another thread's collection by its event check mid-call going through $event and the collect request's dispatch outside docollect's uncount (a lone thread never waits: it collects itself); read per run the fork's event and collect-request counters and correlate with the +96; (b) a one-shot continuation (a lazy seq's, a try's) promoted to multi-shot by another thread's collection and copied on reinstatement (S_promote_to_multishot, the underflow copy); (c) the allocation area's refill after another thread's collection (gc.c:939 drops each thread's tail) charging a header. Not a try: one try/catch entry costs 176 B at stack depths 0, 100, 200 and 400, in bare Jolt and in a Seon test process alike. Ruled out also: contracts, the tick timer, Jolt's runtime hashtables and string-hash cache, Datahike's search and node caches. Correct: a call's bytes are what it allocates, whatever collections land in it and on whatever thread. Proof: one measured call repeated 40 times with another thread allocating gives one byte count.", :changed #{"docs/config.md#constants" ":seon.config.proofs/cost-settled" ":seon.cost/unsettled" "docs/cost.md#what-is-measured" "seon.cost/measure" ":seon.profile/fn-cost" ":seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/cost.md#what-is-measured 60%: A proof measures the function under test, on the thread that calls it, and nothing else in the process (task `seon.cost/a-proof-measures-only-its-own-thread`):" "docs/forks.md#jolt 21%: `reference-code/jolt` pinned at `6e604ac8`, the tip of ``jolt/emit-string-literals``, on `eba8826a`, the tip of ``jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in``, on `2a6ae8f8`, the tip of ``jolt/arrays-compare``, on `86f49de8`, the tip of ``jolt/analysis-lookups``, on ``jolt/record-field-binds`` `0d39edc3`, on `e0351af8` (``jolt/global-ids-pin``: ``jolt/pin-2026-10-23`` `f05de7db` with ``jolt/hashset-bulk`` `ce384f07` merged in)." "docs/storage.md#merges-read-what-changed 15%: A merge decides what merges from the datoms that differ, never by reading whole programs." "docs/failure-points.md#physics-who-may-run-threads 8%: Three rules of the OS and Chez that no code may \"fix\" by adding a thread or a check:" "docs/forks.md#datahike 5%: `reference-code/datahike` pinned at `a9df162d`, the tip of ``jolt/own-suites-on-jolt`` (tests only: `a989ac4a` the core suites load on Jolt, `datahike.test.utils`, `api-test` and `core-test` requiring the hitchhiker tree and `kaocha.stacktrace` only on the JVM; `a9df162d` the tests our commits left stale: `query-cache-test` reads a failed call's message as the cause `6b668f0` wraps, `versioning-test` reads the roster through `branches` (`c0f9149`) and names a transaction id past every branch's (`be25be4`), `gc-test` counts the roster's two records, `query-ir-test` expects Jolt's per-cursor merge; [own suites](#own-suites)), on `e8a2999d`, the tip of ``jolt/attr-compare-mixed-kinds`` (`cmp-attr-nil` compares two attributes of one kind, keywords, entity ids or strings, with no `try`, and any other pair through `safe-compare`: a variable attribute bound to a string, long or symbol is a slice bound no stored attribute compares with, which since `cc614f4e` threw `ClassCastException` where it had joined nothing, and Seon's `[?f ?a ?s]` with `?a` an input in `seon.docs` reaches it."], :functions ["seon.cost/units 4%: Measured costs of unit operations on this runtime (release seon binary, M5 Max, load 5, at 10k units; source mode is within 10% except a plain call, 7 ns; the last five rows were measured in source mode after P2 found :str/append mislabeled: the 308 B per char it showed was the String's seq, not the StringBuilder; :fn/armed-call and the pull rows re-measured on Jolt 00a3b321, J3's try/catch: 990 -> 354 B per armed call), per unit: estimates price their :ops with these." ["seon.cost/measure 3%: f on args, measured at its steady state: calls until `:seon.config.proofs/cost-settled` calls after the one that allocated the fewest bytes so far allocate no fewer; each dimension's minimum over all of them (bytes are the steady state's; ticks and calls their least, which noise only adds to), less what measuring itself costs." [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples :seon.config.proofs/cost-settled :seon.cost/dims :seon.cost/unsettled]] ["seon.merge/readiness 3%: Whether branch db ours can merge into main theirs now, as a merge of main would find it, read only: what main changed since base, their merge base (:main-moved, its program keys), and which of those the branch changed too (:conflicts, the keys a merge of main would leave unmerged)." [:seon.merge/program-key]] "seon.ctx/change-steps 2%: What each step of changes' walk costs, measured once with the contracts on, on a case that isolates it (seon.ctx-test/changes-step-costs-are-current measures them again), at 100 to 1,000 members: the call itself; an entity pair walked into (a member of two keys re-made, less its entries and keys); a collection pair walked into (each side's seq begun: a member holding a one-member collection re-made, less a string's member and two entries); an entity's key and a collection's entry stepped past, each a step of one side as walk-of counts them (keys on members of two to six keys, as a record's are); an element compared by = (the generator's eval records rebuilt equal); and a change (its tuple, and its path printed for the sort)." ["seon.merge/touched-keys 2%: Every program key whose content may differ between base and any of dbs: the keys borne, on base or on that db, by each entity a datom of which one holds and the other does not (datahike.api/datoms-diff, which skips the subtrees they share), when that datom's attribute is one a key's content reads: the program's (program/attr-namespaces) or declared data (data-attr?), never a platform record such as an embedding." [:seon.merge/data-attrs :seon.merge/program-key]]], :schemas [[:seon.merge/result "11%"] [:seon.config.proofs/cost-settled "5%"] [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples "5%"] [:seon.config.render/big-alloc "4%"] [:seon.config.proofs/case-sizes "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.proofs/main-is-proved-in-a-fork", :plan "Owner, 2026-10-27: \"We should be able to cheaply (ms) fork main and run main tests and update the shared test knowledge base for all agents.\" What existed: `seon.prove`'s :main input (the writer's commit notice) only kept main's t, so main was proved only when someone asked (a session's seon_prove, root's gate), and an agent forked from main found none of main's results; a main run spawned a prove agent that then read, load-string'ed and imported every test file of the checkout (`seon.agent/load-tests!`, about 2.2 s + 5.4 s cold for src and test, scratchpad tests-fast.md section 6), though the fork server's main already holds the tests (:seon.config.source/dirs takes test/), and main's run was keyed by the checkout's inputs too (`seon.root/prove!` read the tree through the :source effect). Done: a notice whose ops move the program (`seon.program/moves-program?`, moved here from seon.fork-server, which uses it too) is a request to prove main keyed by its program digest, with no waiter, joined by any session asking for that key; the prove agent forked from main runs at once (the :prepare step deleted); main's key is its program digest as an agent's is. Results are the result-tx facts on main every agent forked after reads (`seon.proofs/stored-results`). Deletes `seon.agent/load-tests!`, `seon.prove/read-digest`, root/prove!'s :source read, and the answer's `:ran true` that hid the run's own :ran count. Not done: digests equal across bin/test and an agent's run (the reuse session's work); process tests stay with bin/prove --merge.", :changed #{"seon.prove/answered" "seon.agent/run-tests-forked"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/merging.md#the-test-service 69%: One proc, `:prove` (```seon.prove/prove-proc```, declared in `resources/seon/system.edn`), admits every request to run tests, whoever asks: a Claude session (the server's `:prove` op, the MCP tool `seon_prove`, ```seon.root/prove!```) and an agent's turn (```seon.a