{:task "seon.problems/health-reads-each-part-once", :plan "`seon.problems/health` computed `answers` and `report` computed it again, and called `problem-unlinked` twice and then `distinct`ed the second pass's repeats of the first: a dedupe of its own double work. Corrected: `report` and `health` share `report-with`, which takes the answers read once; `problem-unlinked` judges its own class's page in its one pass, so the second call and the `distinct` go. Contracted calls of one `health` read on the proof's db: 59 before, 51 after (report-with's own contract is one of them); the rows are unchanged (seon.problems-test/an-issue-with-no-page-is-a-problem and problem-unlinked-follows-the-classes-not-their-rows stay green).", :changed #{"seon.problems/report-with" "seon.problems/run-rows" "seon.problems/problem-unlinked" "docs/health.md#two-kinds-of-problem" "docs/health.md#checks-are-callers-of-seon" "seon.problems/health" "seon.problems/caused-classes" "seon.problems/report" "seon.problems/run-pull"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/health.md#two-kinds-of-problem 49%: A **recorded** problem is one only an effect can observe. Any code reports one by transacting it: an error recorded once per signature (`seon.error`), or a run's red proof, cost verdict, death, conflict, unmerged entry, deferred test or waste written as a fact at its end, a task held for root (`:failed`), and a test's newest red result. `seon.problems/report` reads what was recorded, at a cost that follows the problems and never the program. A run's problems stay open while its task is still to be worked: they close when the task is done or root cancels it. A recorded error closes, with nothing written, once its subject has a fact written after its last occurrence (`seon.problems/closed?`). The subject (`:seon.error/subject`) is a var, a section, or an attribute that only a live proc writes, so its cause being gone is itself the fact. For example, a watcher's death names root's `:seon.root/watch` (`my.root/watch-died-tx`), so the next boot's watcher closes it; a refused read of the task files names `:seon.root/tasks` (```my.root/load-answer```), the outcome of root's last read of them, written with the error in one transaction (```my.root/load-error-tx```) and by every good read (```my.root/tasks-read-tx```). So the first read whose outcome differs closes it, a good one of the same bytes as before the refused one included, while the same refused read again writes the same value, no datom, and it stays open. A subject's fact is the producer's latest outcome: written on success and on failure, never a counter, and an unchanged outcome writes nothing. A read shows one refused file at a time (`seon.task/load-tx` answers the first), so a second file refused later replaces the first's outcome and closes its row; it opens again once the second is fixed and the first is the one shown. An error that names no subject stays open until someone reads it." "docs/errors.md#problems 28%: Every problem Seon recorded is read by one function, ```seon.problems/report```: main's db in, one report out, by class; ```seon.problems/health``` is that report with the classes the program's facts make true now (dead citations, unnamed sites, stale pins and sections, orphan pages, uncited tasks) added." "docs/health.md#health 27%: Health is the set of open problems: a value computed when something reads it (`seon.problems/health`). It is never stored, never precomputed and never polled. Root's page, an agent's context and the done rule each read it on the db they hold. It is the recorded classes (`seon.problems/report`) beside the derived ones (`:dead-citation`, `:unnamed-site`, `:stale-pin`, `:stale-section`, `:orphan-page`, `:leadless-section`, `:uncited-task`, `:problem-unlinked`), one value by class (`:seon.problems/report`)." "docs/health.md#checks-are-callers-of-seon 12%: `bin/check health [CLASS ...]` (`seon.check/health-check`) is the one check of these classes, and it holds a checkout to what a Seon agent is held to: what its branch caused (root's ruling, 2026-10-23)." "docs/root.md#root-causes-at-quiescence 11%: Why released work is not done is observed, never stored on the task: ```seon.reconcile/root-causes``` reads it from the index once, at quiescence."], :functions [["seon.problems/problem-unlinked 5%: Each class of classes ({class {:rows ...}}) with an open row whose wiki section (:seon.problems/docs) is no stored section, or that declares none, as a :problem-unlinked row: an issue that points nowhere is itself a problem, and so is the class of those rows when its own section is missing." [:seon.problems/class :seon.problems/row :seon.section/address]] ["my.root/problems 5%: Every open problem on main, by class (seon.problems/health): recorded errors counted by signature (:platform :eval :agent :effect) and runs' red proofs, cost verdicts, merges, deaths, errors and waste, each row with its producer, owning namespace, evidence as data, reproducing proofs and the tasks already answering it; and what the program's facts make true now (:dead-citation)." [:seon.problems/report :seon.root/sys]] ["seon.problems/caused-split 3%: {:caused :attention :rows}: the derived problems the branch at head has that base (its merge base) had not, by message (:rows: each with its class, both kinds): what the branch's own work broke (:caused: the done rule refuses while any is open, seon.reconcile/done-explain :open-problem) and what it changed under the wiki (:attention: the sections it made stale, which the agent reads and never a reason to refuse; the classes :seon.problems/attention declares)." [:seon.merge/changes :seon.problems/class]] ["seon.problems/unnamed-sites 3%: Each site of a law (`laws`) its page does not name: a live form carrying the law's fact, by the var it defines, in a namespace of Seon's own src (an agent's app and any test are not failure points: a deftest's `is` expands to a catch), whose name no section of the page cites (:seon.section/names), as an :unnamed-site row." [:form/defines :form/id :form/ns :impl/form :impl/protocol :method/form :method/multi :ns/name :seon.problems/row :seon.section/address :seon.section/names :var/name]] ["seon.problems/stale-sections 3%: Each section whose text cites a var whose contract or docstring changed since the section was last written or reviewed, as a :stale-section row naming what changed (:changed), what it was as of then (:was) and what it is (:now): the var's facts (seon.program/meaning) as of the transaction that wrote the section's :seon.section/read (seon.docs/load-tx) or its :seon.section/reviewed (my.docs/reviewed!), read from history, against now." [:seon.problems/row :seon.section/address :seon.section/baseline-unread :seon.section/read :seon.section/reviewed :var/contract :var/doc :var/name]]], :schemas [[:seon.problems/report "31%"] [:seon.problems/class "16%"] [:seon.config.ai/judge-pass-usd "7%"] [:seon.cost/unsettled "4%"] [:seon.turn/not-observed "2%"]]}}
{:task "seon.reconcile/the-index-is-checked-once", :plan "Every reconciler helper (put-task, derive-needs, refresh-ready, refresh-done, idx-ready?, done-info, ...) takes `:seon.reconcile/index`, and its contract checked every entry of the index's map-of and set parts on each call, so listing the index (```seon.reconcile/index``` of a db, an agent's first request build through seon.turn's index!) cost tasks x tasks. Measured on a worktree root's main of 903 tasks: 156M ticks and 636 MB with the contracts on, 26.8M and 149 MB off. The index's parts are checked by kind (:map, set?), and each entry where it enters (put-task's :seon.task/own, put-run's :seon.run/run), as :seon.ctx/ix already was ([cost](docs/cost.md#what-a-contract-costs)): 33.0M ticks and 160 MB after. The `*` pull of a task (seon.task/entity-value) was not a cause: it costs 16k ticks and 90 KB a task, against 41k and 165 KB for a pull naming :seon.task/own's 15 attributes, so it stays.", :changed #{"docs/cost.md#what-a-contract-costs" ":seon.reconcile/state" ":seon.reconcile/index"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt 20%: ``seon.ctx/build`` walks the pattern once, when the agent's state first holds its db (a spawn, a resume, a clone), and ``seon.ctx/patch`` keeps it from each transaction's datoms, which ``seon.agent/transact!`` records with the db they made. The walk's state (`:ix`) knows where each entity is held, its pattern, and which `[attribute value]` each join watches (a reverse join's ref to it, a lookup's name). A datom touches a held entity (its own attributes, a forward join) or a watched join; the patch rebuilds each touched entity where it is held, shortest path first, as a build would, keeping every join and member nothing touched, and copies the path above it. A rebuilt entity keeps every attribute equal to before as the object it was, and a rebuild that changes nothing is the old node. A move with no transaction (a merge of main, a resume) is patched from the datoms the two values differ by. A change of the pattern, or one that moves where an entity is held (a new run reaching the task, the holder leaving its window), builds the record anew, as a fresh build places it, and shares back every subtree it did not change (the same objects): that patch costs the record, once a run." "docs/turn.md#observe 18%: Each turn starts from facts measured now, never remembered: `seon.turn/observe` asks the test service (`seon.agent/prove!`, `seon.prove`: one run per branch and tx whoever asks, in a fork of the agent) to run the task's accumulated proofs and the tests the branch changed, each test bounded by `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` (a run that cannot be made ran no proof, so every proof is unknown and the run's error is `:test-run`), the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (`seon.merge/uncontracted`, the question root's judge asks), its unmerged entries, the problems its own work made true (`seon.problems/caused`: it refuses done), and the done rule over them (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`: [tasks](tasks.md#the-done-rule)), read from the reconciler's index of the branch, which the agent holds across turns and observes from the last turn's db over what differs (`seon.turn/index!`, `seon.reconcile/index`): listing it again every turn cost 92M ticks and 384 MB on a main of 680 tasks, observing it 1.3M and 10 MB." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 9%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)." "docs/vocabulary.md#tasks-task-chains-words 8%: The words for tasks and their prerequisites, and the namespace that owns each." "docs/root.md#runs-and-judging 3%: A run is one agent's try at a task; the reconciler starts it, and judges its end from measured facts only."], :functions [["seon.reconcile/observe 8%: The index after main's commit: ops are the commit's resolved datoms ([op e a v]), db the db after it." [:seon.message/to :seon.reconcile/index :seon.run/branch :seon.run/id :seon.run/task :seon.task/branch :seon.task/id :seon.task/needs :seon.task/proofs]] "seon.fork-server/index-at-fork 8%: Server state st with main's reconciler index (:index {:db :index}, listed when the server starts) observed forward to :db (seon.reconcile/index over what differs), when main moved since: a forked agent's first turn observes its branch from it, never lists the index itself (a listing of 903 tasks: 33M ticks, 160 MB, in every agent)." "seon.reconcile/empty-index 7%: The index of a db with no tasks." ["seon.render.proc/main-info 5%: St with main as db (when newer than the one held): its t, its db, and the reconciler's index of it held and moved by what changed (seon.reconcile/index over the watched datoms that differ: seon.render-cost-test/a-move-of-main-costs-what-moved), whose runs give how each agent's work ended (:agent-run, the words root's process tile uses for its events)." [:seon.run/agent :seon.run/start-ms]] "seon.cost/units 5%: Measured costs of unit operations on this runtime (release seon binary, M5 Max, load 5, at 10k units; source mode is within 10% except a plain call, 7 ns; the last five rows were measured in source mode after P2 found :str/append mislabeled: the 308 B per char it showed was the String's seq, not the StringBuilder; :fn/armed-call and the pull rows re-measured on Jolt 00a3b321, J3's try/catch: 990 -> 354 B per armed call), per unit: estimates price their :ops with these."], :schemas [[:seon.task/own "26%"] [:seon.reconcile/index "21%"] [:seon.run/run "12%"] [:seon.task/task-fact "4%"] [:seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum "4%"]]}}
{:task "datahike.transaction/an-index-insert-allocates-nothing-extra", :plan "Found by root's perf pass, 2026-10-27 (perf-2026-10-27.md): d/db-with of the boot import (~125k datoms) costs 1,914M ticks and 4.88 GB against an estimated need of 350-600M ticks and 50-250 MB. Measured (worktree session an-index-insert-allocates-nothing-extra, seon.profile/measure!): keyword compare (cmp-attr-quick) is not it, 6 ticks and 0 B. One transient :eavt insert is 2.2k ticks and 2.86 KB, 13 comparisons a descent; per comparison, Jolt's == in combine-cmp 32 B (jolt-num-equiv, a rest-args procedure called through its var) and, when e and a match, Datahike's Jolt compare-value 240 B (a try entered, 176 B, and two letfn closures). Fixed at both producers: Jolt jolt/num-equiv-two-args (a 2-arity, 0 B; make numequivalloc) and Datahike jolt/compare-value-no-try (two values of one scalar kind compare with no try). What stays: every interop call on a deftype (.lookup, .cons, .root) walks the method arms with no site cache (task jolt.backend/a-deftype-method-call-takes-a-site-cache), and the import's larger owners are outside the insert: per entity a content id's hash 43 KB (hasch), an identity's upsert ~16 KB, an entity map's expansion ~10 KB (task an-identity-costs-its-seek).", :changed #{"docs/forks.md#jolt" "docs/forks.md#datahike"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/forks.md#jolt 54%: `reference-code/jolt` pinned at `6e604ac8`, the tip of ``jolt/emit-string-literals``, on `eba8826a`, the tip of ``jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in``, on `2a6ae8f8`, the tip of ``jolt/arrays-compare``, on `86f49de8`, the tip of ``jolt/analysis-lookups``, on ``jolt/record-field-binds`` `0d39edc3`, on `e0351af8` (``jolt/global-ids-pin``: ``jolt/pin-2026-10-23`` `f05de7db` with ``jolt/hashset-bulk`` `ce384f07` merged in)." "docs/forks.md#datahike 38%: `reference-code/datahike` pinned at `a9df162d`, the tip of ``jolt/own-suites-on-jolt`` (tests only: `a989ac4a` the core suites load on Jolt, `datahike.test.utils`, `api-test` and `core-test` requiring the hitchhiker tree and `kaocha.stacktrace` only on the JVM; `a9df162d` the tests our commits left stale: `query-cache-test` reads a failed call's message as the cause `6b668f0` wraps, `versioning-test` reads the roster through `branches` (`c0f9149`) and names a transaction id past every branch's (`be25be4`), `gc-test` counts the roster's two records, `query-ir-test` expects Jolt's per-cursor merge; [own suites](#own-suites)), on `e8a2999d`, the tip of ``jolt/attr-compare-mixed-kinds`` (`cmp-attr-nil` compares two attributes of one kind, keywords, entity ids or strings, with no `try`, and any other pair through `safe-compare`: a variable attribute bound to a string, long or symbol is a slice bound no stored attribute compares with, which since `cc614f4e` threw `ClassCastException` where it had joined nothing, and Seon's `[?f ?a ?s]` with `?a` an input in `seon.docs` reaches it. Proof: `datahike.test.query-planner-test/test-variable-attribute-bound-upstream-reads-bound-slices`, red since `cc614f4e`, green; 0.7 B a keyword comparison. Generic), on `219f57f6`, the tip of ``jolt/diff-test-on-jolt-file-store`` (Datahike's `datoms_diff_test` requires `konserve.jolt.store`, so its restored histories run on Jolt's `:file` backend: without it every restored history threw on konserve's JVM store and the property reported the throw as a counterexample; the diff was exact; test only), on `416bdc63`, the tip of ``jolt/a-diff-records-its-reads`` (`65dad766`: `psi/diff` records its `[from to]` range as `-slice` does, so a read built on `d/datoms-diff` under `d/with-reads` is touched by a datom added in the diffed range: `[:a attr]`, or `:any` with no attribute; it recorded nothing, and a render over a diff kept its old answer. Proofs: `datahike.test.datoms-diff-test/a-diff-records-the-range-it-read`, `seon.render-test/a-render-over-a-diff-runs-again-when-the-diff-moves`. Generic), merged with `0d6b58c0`, the tip of ``jolt/compare-value-no-try`` (`d4811ddf`, `0d6b58c0`: `compare-value` on Jolt enters no `try` for two values of one scalar kind, 240 B a comparison that reaches the value before; `compare-data`'s helpers are top-level fns: n refs on one form 38 to 17 KB a datom at 1,000, `seon.db-test/a-value-costs-its-index-inserts`; a type test beside the `try` until Jolt's `try` costs nothing entered, task jolt.backend/a-try-entered-allocates-nothing), on `cc614f4e`, the tip of ``jolt/attribute-compare-without-try`` (`cmp-attr-nil` compares attributes with no `try`), on `e5a55664`, the tip of ``jolt/time-pred-order``, on `872bfa03`, the tip of ``jolt/pull-reverse``, on `336de2ab`, the tip of ``jolt/tx-data-retracts``, on `3fe48c96` (``jolt/multival-lookup-ref``), on `6dc9ce64`, the tip of ``jolt/with-reads`` (`6689077e` records the index ranges a read scanned: `with-reads`, `reads-index`, `datom-keys`, `reads-touched?`; `6dc9ce64` the collector is an atom), on `698a2fa`, the tip of ``jolt/a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error`` (`6b668f0`, `25cc875`, `698a2fa`), on `d89be47` (the tip of ``jolt/datom-cost``: `6db7188` and ``jolt/nested-call-refused`` (`ae35add`, `dda24c0`), each on ``jolt/release-drains`` `bae7c3e` (``jolt/in-scalar`` `1880fcc` merged into it; both on ``jolt/global-ids`` `b4596cf`, ``jolt/gc-cache-epoch`` `700c473` merged in))." "docs/cost.md#how-we-measure 23%: Every measurement, in a cost proof or outside one, starts from a written estimate and is read across sizes, never from one quick run (owner: \"think about things algorithmically first and always put estimates on things\"):" "docs/storage.md#what-a-datom-costs-to-add 21%: `seon.db-test/a-datom-costs-its-index-inserts` says a datom added in memory (`d/db-with`, as every test plan's import is) costs its floor: its inserts into `:eavt` and `:aevt`, one `:eavt` descent for a cardinality-one attribute's current value, the datom and its place in the tx-data, measured at about 6.8k ticks and 10.5 KB on the test store's seed. It costs 7.2k ticks and 11.1 KB. What kept it from its floor was in two dependencies ([forks](forks.md)): a protocol call read every field of the record (Datahike's DB has 21), and Datahike found the current value through a slice's seq and copied the DB record for writes that change nothing. An identity costs more than its `:avet` insert and seek should (`seon.db-test/an-identity-costs-its-seek`, an accepted overrun on its task): 11.5k ticks and 34 KB per datom for a `:db/unique` one, 16.9k and 65 KB for a content id." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 9%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)."], :functions ["seon.cost/units 7%: Measured costs of unit operations on this runtime (release seon binary, M5 Max, load 5, at 10k units; source mode is within 10% except a plain call, 7 ns; the last five rows were measured in source mode after P2 found :str/append mislabeled: the 308 B per char it showed was the String's seq, not the StringBuilder; :fn/armed-call and the pull rows re-measured on Jolt 00a3b321, J3's try/catch: 990 -> 354 B per armed call), per unit: estimates price their :ops with these." ["seon.program/program-digest 4%: The digest of db's program as a whole: its live forms (:form/id, hasch of a form's namespace and text, docs/storage.md#ids: a replaced form is retracted), its schema entries (:schema/form) and its namespaces' tails, each by its entity, a content id of its identity (docs/storage.md#ids), so the index's order is the same on every branch and nothing is sorted." [:form/id :ns/tail :schema/form]] ["seon.cost/run 3%: Estimate e measured: its function on its input at each size (sizes, under :seon.config.proofs/cost-max-n), ascending, or on each generated case (`cases`) against its contract's bound on that case's arguments (`bound`, kept on the row as :args), stopping after the first point over its estimate in an exact dimension." [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-n :seon.cost/estimate :seon.cost/row :seon.cost/too-few-sizes :seon.cost/verdict]] ["seon.root/prove! 2%: Ask the test service (seon.prove) to prove a branch at its head, the tests of namespaces names (a set of symbols; none: all it selects): agent id's own branch in mode :turn (as its turn proves it; mode :judge, as root's judge does), or main (agent nil) in mode :main (every test with no result at its digest: seon.agent/run-tests-forked)." [:seon.agent/id :seon.root/sys]] ["seon.problems/caused-split 2%: {:caused :attention :rows}: the derived problems the branch at head has that base (its merge base) had not, by message (:rows: each with its class, both kinds): what the branch's own work broke (:caused: the done rule refuses while any is open, seon.reconcile/done-explain :open-problem) and what it changed under the wiki (:attention: the sections it made stale, which the agent reads and never a reason to refuse; the classes :seon.problems/attention declares)." [:seon.merge/changes :seon.problems/class]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum "13%"] [:seon.config.store/value-out-of-line-bytes "4%"] [:seon.config.ai/judge-pass-usd "4%"] [:seon.db/ref "3%"] [:seon.root/end "2%"]]}}
{:task "seon.reconcile/an-index-is-observed-at-the-cost-of-what-changed", :plan "Observing main's index (```seon.reconcile/index``` with the index of an earlier db, seon.turn's index!) takes a datahike.api/datoms-diff of each of the 84 watched attributes' ranges. Its proof measured it on the test store, whose in-memory values keep no node at an address, so each diff walked its attribute's datoms: 4.71M ticks and 17.9 MB at 10 tasks, 5.36M and 18.3 MB at 500, a fixed part no root's main has and one that hid the work below n=1,000 (an earlier build: 8.2M ticks and 64 MB, most of it a slice comparator's try, since fixed in Datahike cc614f4e). The proof's input is a store of its own on disk per size (seon.reconcile-test/observed-input, the one-store rule's last resort, said where it is made): on LMDB the observe reads 1.11M ticks and 5.8 MB at 10, 1.03M and 4.6 MB at 500, 1.47M and 5.8 MB at 1,000, each unchanged attribute one cached descent (5-11k ticks). The 84 descents left are task seon.reconcile/one-observe-descends-once.", :changed #{"docs/cost.md#how-we-measure"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/cost.md#how-we-measure 56%: Every measurement, in a cost proof or outside one, starts from a written estimate and is read across sizes, never from one quick run (owner: \"think about things algorithmically first and always put estimates on things\"):" "docs/turn.md#observe 23%: Each turn starts from facts measured now, never remembered: `seon.turn/observe` asks the test service (`seon.agent/prove!`, `seon.prove`: one run per branch and tx whoever asks, in a fork of the agent) to run the task's accumulated proofs and the tests the branch changed, each test bounded by `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` (a run that cannot be made ran no proof, so every proof is unknown and the run's error is `:test-run`), the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (`seon.merge/uncontracted`, the question root's judge asks), its unmerged entries, the problems its own work made true (`seon.problems/caused`: it refuses done), and the done rule over them (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`: [tasks](tasks.md#the-done-rule)), read from the reconciler's index of the branch, which the agent holds across turns and observes from the last turn's db over what differs (`seon.turn/index!`, `seon.reconcile/index`): listing it again every turn cost 92M ticks and 384 MB on a main of 680 tasks, observing it 1.3M and 10 MB. That is all a turn observes: the done rule's inputs. What else a reader wants of the branch (whether it can merge, what its merge carries, what it changed under the wiki, its tests) is a view of the task's render (```seon.render.ui/merge-view```, `divergence-view`, `caused-view`, `gaps-view`), a component over the branch's db with main's and the merge base's commits as its children, drawn when one of them moves, never per turn (owner, 2026-10-27: \"Info that is derived and made for the agent or the user should go into the hiccup\"). Merge readiness per turn read main's whole diff (1.78B ticks with main moved); a turn's observe now costs 9.4M ticks (measured on an outside agent, nothing new), and an unrelated eval redraws none of the views (`seon.render-test/an-unrelated-change-redraws-none-of-the-merge-views`)." "docs/forks.md#datahike 18%: `reference-code/datahike` pinned at `a9df162d`, the tip of ``jolt/own-suites-on-jolt`` (tests only: `a989ac4a` the core suites load on Jolt, `datahike.test.utils`, `api-test` and `core-test` requiring the hitchhiker tree and `kaocha.stacktrace` only on the JVM; `a9df162d` the tests our commits left stale: `query-cache-test` reads a failed call's message as the cause `6b668f0` wraps, `versioning-test` reads the roster through `branches` (`c0f9149`) and names a transaction id past every branch's (`be25be4`), `gc-test` counts the roster's two records, `query-ir-test` expects Jolt's per-cursor merge; [own suites](#own-suites)), on `e8a2999d`, the tip of ``jolt/attr-compare-mixed-kinds`` (`cmp-attr-nil` compares two attributes of one kind, keywords, entity ids or strings, with no `try`, and any other pair through `safe-compare`: a variable attribute bound to a string, long or symbol is a slice bound no stored attribute compares with, which since `cc614f4e` threw `ClassCastException` where it had joined nothing, and Seon's `[?f ?a ?s]` with `?a` an input in `seon.docs` reaches it." "docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt 9%: ``seon.ctx/build`` walks the pattern once, when the agent's state first holds its db (a spawn, a resume, a clone), and ``seon.ctx/patch`` keeps it from each transaction's datoms, which ``seon.agent/transact!`` records with the db they made." "docs/storage.md#ids 4%: Every entity id and every transaction id is issued once across the whole store: every branch, every process."], :functions ["seon.reconcile/empty-index 5%: The index of a db with no tasks." ["seon.reconcile/observe 4%: The index after main's commit: ops are the commit's resolved datoms ([op e a v]), db the db after it." [:seon.message/to :seon.reconcile/index :seon.run/branch :seon.run/id :seon.run/task :seon.task/branch :seon.task/id :seon.task/needs :seon.task/proofs]] ["seon.render.proc/main-info 4%: St with main as db (when newer than the one held): its t, its db, and the reconciler's index of it held and moved by what changed (seon.reconcile/index over the watched datoms that differ: seon.render-cost-test/a-move-of-main-costs-what-moved), whose runs give how each agent's work ended (:agent-run, the words root's process tile uses for its events)." [:seon.run/agent :seon.run/start-ms]] ["seon.fork-server/server-step 3%: The fork server's step-fn." [:seon.config.root/system-path :seon.step/step-fn]] "seon.fork-server/index-at-fork 3%: Server state st with main's reconciler index (:index {:db :index}, listed when the server starts) observed forward to :db (seon.reconcile/index over what differs), when main moved since: a forked agent's first turn observes its branch from it, never lists the index itself (a listing of 903 tasks: 33M ticks, 160 MB, in every agent)."], :schemas [[:seon.config.store/value-out-of-line-bytes "9%"] [:seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes "7%"] [:seon.config.agent/test-ticks "6%"] [:seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes "6%"] [:seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.watch/root-watches-what-its-loaders-read", :plan "Found 2026-10-26 (root's question: fseventsd, pid 81742, has held a core since about 10-23). Measured, read-only: fseventsd uses ~62 CPU-seconds a minute at ~127 file events a second system-wide (a 20 s FSEvents stream on /: 2,540 events), about 8 ms of its CPU an event, so it is saturated: today's watcher runs measured deliveries 5 to 18 s late under load. Its cumulative CPU (6,566 min in 12.4 days since its start on 10-14, 37% on average) means the pinned stretch began around 10-22/23, which is when seon.watch arrived (3565b72e45, 10-23), not when fseventsd started. What Seon asks of it: my.root/watch! watches the whole checkout and the repository's refs with per-file events (native/watch.c: FileEvents, NoDefer, WatchRoot, latency 0). For the main tree that checkout holds every worktree (.worktrees/*), each with target/ (builds, shards, check runs, lmdb-sem's 65,536-file search), runs/ (LMDB stores) and .git/modules: of the 20 s sample, ~85% of the volume's events were under /Users/sean/src/seon-next, and every one is delivered to the main root's stream, though watch-loads reads none of them (a batch no loader reads writes nothing). Every test root and watch test starts a stream of its own over its checkout too: 9,673 new fseventsd clients in the 6 hours to 20:30 (3,454 and 4,308 in the two busiest hours, much of it this session's watcher loops), each, since d28e10a585, starting from an event id, so fseventsd runs a history scan (scan_old) for each. Not proven: how fseventsd's per-event cost depends on its clients (removed clients are not logged, and its client list needs root), so whether stale clients add to it is open. Correct, at the cause: root watches what its loaders read, my.root/loader-dirs (docs, the task dir, the source dirs) and the refs, never the checkout, so the churn of builds, stores and other worktrees never reaches a stream; watch-loads' :rescan and :root handling is unchanged (a rescan above a loader dir still reads it). Measured before and after, on the same machine: fseventsd's CPU a minute and the events delivered to the main root's stream a minute (its :seon.root/watch fact's :paths), and a watcher's ready-to-delivery lag. The owner decides whether fseventsd is restarted for a clean baseline. Replaces nothing; deletes the checkout root from watch!.", :changed #{"seon.main/root-serve" "my.root/watch!" "my.root/watch-loads" "seon.watch/start!" "docs/health.md#every-writer-root-loads-what-changed" "my.root/loader"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/health.md#every-writer-root-loads-what-changed 79%: Root watches what its loaders read (the wiki, the task files, the source dirs: `my.root/loader-dirs`) and its repository's refs, never the whole checkout, whose builds, stores and other worktrees no loader reads, so whoever writes (a Claude session, a Seon agent, the owner's editor, git) reaches the same facts with no script of their own (owner, 2026-10-23: \"seon code detecting and updating, not external scripts\")." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 12%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check." "docs/machine.md#builds-and-checks 5%: Builds need a C compiler and make (the Xcode command line tools), git, curl and cmake (llama.cpp's build, `bin/build embed`, [vectors](vector.md#the-model)); nothing else is installed, and every download lands under `target/` (the embedding model once, in the main tree's `target/cache`)." "docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-robust-locks 4%: On macOS, LMDB locks with POSIX semaphores unless built with ``MDB_USE_ROBUST`` (LMDB's `reference-code/lmdb/libraries/liblmdb/mdb.c`)." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 4%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data."], :functions [["my.root/watch! 18%: Root watches what its loaders read, loader-dirs (the wiki, the task files, the source dirs), and its repository's refs (seon.watch, FSEvents: every writer, a Claude session, an agent, the owner's editor, git), never the whole checkout, whose builds (target/), store (runs/) and, in the main tree, other worktrees no loader reads." [:seon.root/id :seon.root/sys :seon.root/watch]] "my.root/watch-loads 4%: What a watcher's batch makes root load, each kind with the first record of the batch that made it ({kind [record-kind path]}: what root's watch fact keeps as :last-load, so a load says why): a changed path by its loader; the checkout's own branch or the source root's moving (own, their refs under refs: a commit or merge on either) the source, and no other ref (another worktree's commit is not this root's); a rescan or a moved root (FSEvents coalesced or dropped what happened under it) the loaders reading under that path (a rescan of a loader's dir that loader), and of refs the source alone." ["my.root/watch-died-tx 4%: The write of watcher error e at ms on main db, with held root's current :seon.root/watch fact: a :platform error (seon.root/platform-tx) whose subject is the watch fact, and that fact with its :error, in one transaction." [:seon.error/message :seon.error/subject :seon.root/id :seon.root/watch]] "my.root/loader 3%: Which of main's loaders reads path (absolute) under checkout co: :docs (a page, .md, under the wiki's dir), :tasks (a task file, .edn, under the task dir, which a test root keeps outside its checkout), :source (under a source dir), or nil: the dirs are loader-dirs, the ones watched." ["seon.root/import-source! 3%: Main's program follows Seon's own source (seon.source): root's dirs read through the :source effect (first brought to the main checkout's branch, seon.fx/read-source: fast-forwarded when behind, a recorded gap when it holds commits main lacks; their files, the tree's HEAD commit and its reference-code pins, and the tree's inputs with no program facts, seon.fx/inputs)." [:form/id :ns/file :schema/file :seon.error/message :seon.error/subject :seon.root/id :seon.root/source]]], :schemas [[:seon.root/watch "28%"] [:seon.config.root/tasks-dir "13%"] [:seon.task/state "7%"] [:seon.watch/died "7%"] [:seon.lib/unwatched "5%"]]}}
{:task "seon.cost/a-contract-carries-its-cost-bound", :plan "The owner's direction (2026-10-26, after notes/cost-bounds-2026-10-26.md): a design writes data, the backbone functions declared with their contracts and bounds before their bodies exist, and an implement task is done when every declaration holds. Step 1: `:seon.cost/bound` is a contract key beside `:malli/schema`, (fn [& args] {:ticks :bytes :calls :out}), read by ```seon.cost/bound```. A cost proof gains a generated form (`:seon.cost/generated`): ```seon.cost/cases``` draws argument vectors from a test.check generator or the contract's input (malli), at the test's seed, and ```seon.cost/run``` measures each against the bound on the same arguments, judged by ```seon.cost/judge``` at the same slack. Growth is not fitted, since the bound states it. This is one mechanism: the same run, measure and judge, with no second runner. Proved on ```seon.ctx/changes```, whose bound sketches its walk (```seon.ctx/walk-of```) priced at per-step costs measured once (docs/cost.md#a-bound-in-the-contract), and a copy without its identical? skip is red under the same bound. Step 2 follows on its own branch: declaring a var with its contract and bound and no body from the REPL, visible in ctx, and the bound-graph render.", :changed #{"seon.cost/per-unit" "seon.cost/explain" "docs/cost.md#a-bound-in-the-contract" "seon.cost/proof" "seon.cost/growth" "seon.cost/expected" "seon.cost/bound" "seon.ctx/change-steps" ":seon.config.proofs/case-sizes" ":seon.cost/estimate" ":seon.cost/sized" ":seon.cost/row" ":seon.cost/no-bound" "seon.ctx/changes-bound" "seon.cost/run" "docs/resolution.md#sanctioned" "seon.ctx/walk-of" ":seon.cost/generated" "seon.cost/cases" "seon.ctx/changes" "seon.cost/judge" ":seon.cost/unit"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/resolution.md#sanctioned 50%: The sites that resolve a name held in data to a var by design, each with its reason." "docs/cost.md#how-we-measure 14%: Every measurement, in a cost proof or outside one, starts from a written estimate and is read across sizes, never from one quick run (owner: \"think about things algorithmically first and always put estimates on things\"):" "docs/cost.md#how-growth-is-judged 6%: Ticks allow one timer chunk." "docs/cost.md#cost-proofs 5%: **Cost is work done (ticks, bytes, calls), never wall or CPU time** (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I like ticks vs wall clock or anything that is load dependent." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 5%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)."], :functions ["seon.cost/exact 9%: The dimensions a verdict is decided on: counted, so the same code on the same input measures the same on any machine at any load, and a green is a function of its reach digest (AGENTS.md section 5, results reused by input digest)." ["seon.cost/measure 5%: f on args, measured at its steady state: calls until `:seon.config.proofs/cost-settled` calls after the one that allocated the fewest bytes so far allocate no fewer; each dimension's minimum over all of them (bytes are the steady state's; ticks and calls their least, which noise only adds to), less what measuring itself costs." [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples :seon.config.proofs/cost-settled :seon.cost/dims :seon.cost/unsettled]] "seon.contract/checked 5%: f checked by contract form c (compiled once, here: seon.contract/wrap), errors named fn-sym; f itself when instrumentation is off." "seon.cost/units 5%: Measured costs of unit operations on this runtime (release seon binary, M5 Max, load 5, at 10k units; source mode is within 10% except a plain call, 7 ns; the last five rows were measured in source mode after P2 found :str/append mislabeled: the 308 B per char it showed was the String's seq, not the StringBuilder; :fn/armed-call and the pull rows re-measured on Jolt 00a3b321, J3's try/catch: 990 -> 354 B per armed call), per unit: estimates price their :ops with these." "seon.cost/dimensions 4%: The measured dimensions, in the order a verdict shows them."], :schemas [[:seon.config.proofs/case-sizes "33%"] [:seon.cost/no-bound "28%"] [:seon.cost/generated "14%"] [:seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum "6%"] [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples "5%"]]}}
{:task "seon.fork-server/every-fork-server-wait-ends-with-the-server", :plan "Found 2026-10-25 by the stability sweep (notes/stability-waits-2026-10-25.md), checked against the code. A wait on the fork server ends only on its answer. (1) `seon.fork-server/promote` with no live standby answers :fork-server-lost and keeps :waiters: each queued :ext request (:stats, :static) is never answered, and a request arriving after the loss is queued with nothing to send it to (:ext sends only when :pid). The loss must answer every waiter, and every later request, with the declared error (docs/failure-points.md, the fork server). A fork asked after the loss is the same wait: the :cmd fork was neither sent nor answered, and the attach that asked for it hung (an agent test's root whose fork server died at boot, add-deps refusing a jar). Both answer at once: a waiter with `seon.fork-server/lost` (:seon.root/refused), a fork with :fork-failed naming it, its socket end closed. The other two waits are `seon.fork-server/an-answer-pairs-by-request-and-stop-ends-a-wedged-server`.", :changed #{"seon.fork-server/lost" "docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data" "seon.root/back-op" "seon.fork-server/promote" "seon.fork-server/release!" "seon.fork-server/fork-server-proc"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data 59%: The failure points met without a catch, where the edge answers with data." "docs/failure-points.md#physics-who-may-run-threads 25%: Three rules of the OS and Chez that no code may \"fix\" by adding a thread or a check:" "docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-robust-locks 20%: On macOS, LMDB locks with POSIX semaphores unless built with ``MDB_USE_ROBUST`` (LMDB's `reference-code/lmdb/libraries/liblmdb/mdb.c`)." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 14%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data." "docs/forks.md#jolt 11%: `reference-code/jolt` pinned at `6e604ac8`, the tip of ``jolt/emit-string-literals``, on `eba8826a`, the tip of ``jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in``, on `2a6ae8f8`, the tip of ``jolt/arrays-compare``, on `86f49de8`, the tip of ``jolt/analysis-lookups``, on ``jolt/record-field-binds`` `0d39edc3`, on `e0351af8` (``jolt/global-ids-pin``: ``jolt/pin-2026-10-23`` `f05de7db` with ``jolt/hashset-bulk`` `ce384f07` merged in)."], :functions [["seon.fork-server/fork-server-proc 9%: The fork server's side in root, and the one writer of its command pipe." [:seon.config.root/standby-every :seon.error/message :seon.step/step-fn]] ["seon.root/ext-op 4%" [:seon.config.root/max-agents :seon.fx/unknown-kind :seon.root/api-state :seon.root/request]] "seon.fork-server/request-standby 3%: Ask the current server to fork a standby; its socket to root goes with the request (SCM_RIGHTS), root keeps the other end." ["seon.root/back-op 3%: A message from an agent proc, a fork-server or writer event, or Flow's error channel." [:seon.config.root/view-window :seon.error/message :seon.root/api-state :seon.root/proc-defect :seon.task/cause :seon.task/id]] ["seon.fork-server/server-step 3%: The fork server's step-fn." [:seon.config.root/system-path :seon.step/step-fn]]], :schemas [[:seon.root/refused "18%"] [:seon.task/state "6%"] [:seon.test/fork-refused "5%"] [:seon.config.agent/stall-ms "5%"] [:seon.root/quiet-before-true "4%"]]}}
{:task "datahike.db/a-time-predicate-read-keeps-index-order", :plan "Done (Datahike e5a55664, jolt/time-pred-order). Found by worktree session ctx-atom (2026-10-26) while building pull's :reverse option (Datahike 872bfa03, docs/forks.md#datahike). A db read through a time predicate (as-of, since) answers its datoms out of index order once a read spans more than eight [e a] groups, and its rseek-datoms realizes the index from the seek point down to the index's start. Reproduction, on a :keep-history? store: an entity root and ten children, each written in its own transaction with [:parent root]; as of the sixth child's transaction, (d/datoms asof :avet :parent root) answers six datoms, and (take 6 (d/rseek-datoms asof :avet :parent root)) answers [7 9 9 \"c5\" 6 …], out of order and leaving the [:parent root] prefix (with four children both answer in order). Cause: datahike.db/post-process-datoms, for a context with a time predicate and not historical, transduces the datoms into a map grouped by [e a] (group-by-step) and assembles each group's current datoms (assemble-datoms-xform): Clojure's array map keeps insertion order up to eight entries and a hash map after, so the answer's order is the hash's; the transduce is eager, so over rseek-datoms' open range (down to the index's start) it reads everything below the seek point. Anything reading an as-of or since db in index order (a pull's collection, a seek, a newest-n window, a merge of two sorted reads) can be wrong. Correct, in Datahike on a jolt/<topic> branch: a time-predicate read keeps index order (each [e a] group's current datoms emitted where the group starts, in the order the groups were read) and stays lazy, so a seek or rseek reads what it is taken for. Pull's :reverse sorts the bounded prefix on such a db (datahike.pull-api/reverse-datoms), so its :limit n there reads the whole collection; this task deletes that sort branch and its datahike.datom require, leaving rseek-datoms the one read. Proof: datahike.test.time-predicate-order-test/a-time-predicate-read-keeps-index-order (ten children as of the sixth: datoms and rseek-datoms in index order, rseek ends at the prefix), and Datahike's own suite answering the same before and after.", :changed #{"docs/forks.md#datahike"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/ctx.md#the-pattern 61%: The map is the agent's entity pulled by a pattern: Datahike's pull syntax, held as data (``seon.render/pattern`` reads it, for the walk and for the page's units alike). An entity schema declares its record's pattern in its `:seon.ctx/pattern` property (the agent's, a run's, a task's, a var's, root's), and an agent edits its own by `swap!`ing `:seon.ctx/pattern` on ctx: the edit is an attribute of its entity, written as any `swap!` is, and the record is built again by it (``seon.ctx/pattern-of``). Nothing else says what ctx holds." "docs/storage.md#ids 22%: Every entity id and every transaction id is issued once across the whole store: every branch, every process. So a datom names the same entity and the same transaction wherever it travels, onto main by a merge or into a query over several branches. The ids come from one page every process writing the store shares (```seon.db/ids!```, `native/ids.c`): two 64-bit words, the last entity id and the last transaction id issued, mapped from the file `ids` beside `data.mdb` (an anonymous page for a store in memory). A process forked from root (the fork server, an agent, a clone) maps the same page, so it needs nothing at fork. Datahike takes them through its id source (`datahike.ids`, `datahike.writing/set-ids!`):" "docs/storage.md#values-out-of-line 21%: A datom value at least `:seon.config.store/value-out-of-line-bytes` large (1,024, the default; `:never` keeps every value inline) is stored once under its own key inside Datahike's store, and the index nodes hold a reference to it. Datahike's `:index-config :value-out-of-line-bytes` does this in the node storage of [our fork](forks.md), ``datahike.index.persistent-set/CachedStorage``:" "docs/storage.md#collection 21%: Root collects the store while it is the store's only writer: at quiescence, with no agent live, and only when no other root writes the store. The reconciler asks once per quiet spell (```seon.reconcile/quiet```, which also records the spell on main as `:seon.root/quiescent` and does not await either answer), root refuses while an agent is live (`seon.root/api-proc`, `:collect`), and the writer runs ```seon.db/collect!```:" "docs/storage.md#plain-values 14%: A value that is plain data (a map, vector, set or list of strings, numbers, keywords, symbols, uuids and instants) is stored as data: its attribute is ``:db.type/data`` in [our Datahike](forks.md), and the store's serializer (jfasl) writes it with no text in between."], :functions ["seon.ctx/members 6%: The entities join j holds of entity e (m, its own attributes), in the order ctx holds them: a reverse join's by when each joined, newest first with :reverse, its :limit the newest n (ids issued in time order are read by rseek and stop there, docs/forks.md :reverse; content-derived ids are read whole and ordered by the transaction that wrote each ref); a forward one's by its values, a :by join's each symbol as [by v] (a symbol naming no entity joins nothing)." ["seon.ctx/window-moves 5%: Join j's new members (r: the record's state) from moves, the datoms that touched it, when j is a window of ids issued in time order (rseek's newest first) and old holds its members: [v r entered], each member a retraction names and no assertion brings back dissoc'd (forgotten), each an assertion names that it does not hold assoc'd (built), the oldest past its limit dropped, a window left short by one that left filled from below its oldest by rseek, and the ids it built; nil when moves are not the join's own ref datoms (its full read answers then)." [:seon.ctx/ix]] "seon.ctx/in-time-order? 5%: Do the ids of the entities reverse join j reaches grow with time: issued by the store's counter, not derived from their content (:seon.db/content-id on their identity)? Then their ids' order is the order they were written." ["seon.ctx/join 4%: Join j of entity e (m, its own attributes) at path p: [value r], value its members' nodes (a collection: a map by their ids, newest first; else the one node), r the record's state gaining their paths and the join's watch keys." [:seon.ctx/ix]] ["seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc 4%: The reconciler." [:seon.run/status :seon.step/step-fn]]], :schemas [[:seon.task/state "4%"] [:seon.reconcile/step "3%"] [:seon.ctx/ix "3%"] [:seon.config.root/wiki-baseline "3%"] [:seon.reconcile/index "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.db/a-store-is-made-whole-or-not-at-all", :plan "Found by the full run of 2026-10-26 (main 6929ca4e9, shard-0): a store's create that failed after its LMDB env had made data.mdb (MDB_SEM_UNDO_FULL, the env's first write) left a file holding no database at the path. `seon.db/open!` takes the data file for the store (exists?), so it never creates it again, and every later open connects and reads \"Database does not exist\"; konserve refuses to create over the file (\"LMDB store already exists\"), so no open recovers it. Correct (root's ruling, 2026-10-26): the create is atomic. `seon.db/open!` builds a new store in a sibling temporary directory (the database created, seeded and synced there, its connection released so its env closes) and renames it to the path: a rename is atomic on one filesystem, so the path holds a whole store or none. A failed create leaves only its temporary directory; two creators race only on the rename, and the loser removes its own and opens the winner's. A store is named by the id it records, never its path (`seon.db/store-cfg`, `seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens`), so the renamed store opens at the path. This removes the half-made state rather than recovering from it; docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-robust-locks's manual removal goes. Proof: seon.db-test/a-create-that-fails-leaves-no-store, a create made to fail after its env file exists (the process's undo entries exhausted, so the env opens and its first write is refused): red, the path holds a data file and the next open reads \"Database does not exist\"; green, the path is missing and the next open creates a whole store.", :changed #{":seon.db/store-unplaced" "seon.db/make!" "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant" "docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-robust-locks" "seon.db/open!"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-robust-locks 72%: On macOS, LMDB locks with POSIX semaphores unless built with ``MDB_USE_ROBUST`` (LMDB's `reference-code/lmdb/libraries/liblmdb/mdb.c`).\nThose are not robust: a process SIGKILLed inside a write transaction leaves the writer lock\nheld, and every other process with the env open (root always has its store open) blocks on\nits next write, forever. Measured by `target/lmdb-kill` (`native/lmdb_kill.c`):\nwith the POSIX build the next writer and the holder both block; with the robust build both\ncommit and the killed transaction is gone. `bin/build lmdb` builds `target/lib/liblmdb.dylib`\nrobust from `reference-code/lmdb` (stamped with its commit), `bin/env` points `JOLT_LMDB_LIB`\nat it, and `bin/check`'s `lmdb-kill` step proves it. Every process on a store must load the\nsame build: the lock scheme is part of the lock file's format." "docs/storage.md#identity 47%: A store is named by the id it records, never by where it lies, so a kept run opens wherever it is copied, moved or archived. ```seon.db/store-cfg``` reads the id from the store's `:db` head (```seon.db/stored-id```, Datahike's own read path, 0.8 ms) and mints `(hasch/uuid path)` only for a store it is about to make (`seon.db/open!` creates that store from the config ```seon.db/made-cfg``` derives); Datahike's connect refuses a config whose id differs from the stored one (`:store-identity-mismatch`). A copy keeps its original's id, as a Datahike replica does, so one process opens one of them. A connection's db names the store where it lies: our Datahike's `datahike.connector/deref-conn` keeps the connection's own config, as connect does, so `(:config @conn)` after a move names the new path (task `seon.db/deref-keeps-the-connections-config`). Proof: `seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens`." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 22%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data." "docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data 10%: The failure points met without a catch, where the edge answers with data." "docs/forks.md#lmdb 8%: `reference-code/lmdb` pinned at `15420ce`, the tip of ``jolt/sysv-sem-identity``, built from source by `bin/build lmdb` with `MDB_USE_ROBUST` ([failure points](failure-points.md#lmdb-robust-locks))."], :functions [["seon.db/make! 19%: A store made whole at path, or none (task seon.db/a-store-is-made-whole-or-not-at-all): built in a sibling directory, path.making-<uuid> (created, seeded with about n datoms, synced, its connection released so its env closes), then renamed to path, which is atomic on one filesystem." [:seon.db/store-unplaced :seon.error/fix :seon.error/message]] "seon.db/MDB_NOSYNC 5%" "seon.db/writer-cfg 4%" ["seon.db/ids! 4%: Make the store of conn take its entity ids and transaction ids from one page every process writing it shares (native/ids.c): the file `ids` beside a durable store, or an anonymous page for a store in memory; a process forked from this one maps the same page." [:seon.db/ids-unopened :seon.error/fix :seon.error/message]] ["seon.root/boot! 3%: Root's boot up to its first thread, on the main carrier (jolt.host/main-carrier-run!: one OS thread drives every fiber, so a go block or future a library starts is a fiber and nothing else runs): main opened on the store at path, its indexes preloaded, its program loaded and instrumented, the warm paths run and the heap made static, then the fork server forked from it, sharing all of that copy-on-write while this is the one thread there is (docs/failure-points.md, physics)." [:seon.config.root/max-agents :seon.db/branch]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes "15%"] [:seon.task/state "10%"] [:seon.config.store/value-out-of-line-bytes "6%"] [:seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes "6%"] [:seon.db/store-unplaced "4%"]]}}
