{:task "my.agent/a-name-that-is-no-var-says-so", :plan "The live rounds (notes/live-2026-10-25.md, rounds 4-9: what still fails) ended runs :no-progress on calls whose answers gave nothing new, among them `(my.agent/callers 'seon.proof/status)`: a symbol where the registry holds the key `:seon.proof/status`. ```my.agent/callers``` and ```my.agent/callees``` took the symbol as it was and answered [] for a name that is no var, which reads as no callers (AGENTS section 9: unknown is not empty), so the agent asked again. Now a symbol that names no var on the branch nor in the process (a library's var is one) answers :my.agent/no-var, naming it, with the form for the registry key of that name when the branch has one (an attribute, a config key or a schema entry). Replaces the silent [].", :changed #{"my.agent/callees" "my.agent/callers" ":my.agent/no-var" ":seon.turn/not-observed" "docs/resolution.md#sanctioned" "my.agent/no-var" "my.agent/asked"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt 34%: ``seon.ctx/build`` walks the pattern once, when the agent's state first holds its db (a spawn, a resume, a clone), and ``seon.ctx/patch`` keeps it from each transaction's datoms, which ``seon.agent/transact!`` records with the db they made. The walk's state (`:ix`) knows where each entity is held, its pattern, and which `[attribute value]` each join watches (a reverse join's ref to it, a lookup's name). A datom touches a held entity (its own attributes, a forward join) or a watched join; the patch rebuilds each touched entity where it is held, shortest path first, as a build would, keeping every join and member nothing touched, and copies the path above it. A rebuilt entity keeps every attribute equal to before as the object it was, and a rebuild that changes nothing is the old node. A move with no transaction (a merge of main, a resume) is patched from the datoms the two values differ by. A change of the pattern, or one that moves where an entity is held (a