up at run time is one it reaches, so a test that resolves its var by name is no green reused after that var changes, or once it comes to exist. Any other name a form holds as data (quoted: `tree-readers`, `env-readers`, `seon.config/schema-forms`, whose `:seon.render/*` properties name every render, a form sent to an agent) is no reference, so reaching that form is neither using nor reaching what it names (below)." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 24%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check. The merge's proof is `bin/prove` on the merged tree, then `bin/check smoke`; `bin/laws` (every law's sites) and `health` ([health](health.md)) run inside it as steps keyed by the whole tree. One rule selects for everyone: a key runs exactly when no green result is stored at its reach digest (```seon.proofs/plan```), in the one results directory every checkout shares (`:seon.config.source/test-results-dir`), so an unchanged tree runs nothing and a result proven in any checkout is reused in every other." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 19%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data." "docs/source.md#mains-program-follows-the-tree 16%: Main's program is the tree's: at each boot, when the files read differently from main's last import (their digest differs from the fact's `:digest`, or none is recorded), main's program is replaced by the tree's in one transaction, ```seon.source/replace-tx```; when they read the same, nothing is imported or written."], :functions [["seon.agent/test-plan 7%: What judges branch db, as this process holds it: seon.proofs/plan of its tests' reach digests (seon.program/reach-digests) against base's (main at its merge base), the greens stored at those digests (files in results-dir, and the test service's result facts in db, greens and reds: seon.proofs/stored-results) and proofs." [:form/src :ns/form :ns/name :seon.config.source/test-results-dir :var/form :var/kind :var/name]] ["seon.agent/run-tests-forked 6%: Fork this agent; the child runs what test-plan runs for proofs (the selected tests with no green held at their digest), each test within ticks engine ticks of work of its own, writing each test var as it starts and then one EDN line of its result on a pipe." [:seon.error/message :seon.test/fork-refused :seon.test/result]] "seon.program/config-key-readers 5%: The vars that read a config key named in data rather than in their own code: a form defining one reads every config key a schema entry's form names (schema-config-keys), so a test reaching it takes those keys' values (config-reads names the rest)." ["seon.writer/other-op 5%: Every request but :tx." [:seon.config.store/sole-writer? :seon.root/collected :seon.root/id :seon.writer/request :seon.writer/state]] "seon.program/reach-update 5%: Graph g (reach-graph's, of another value) for db: the live forms g holds that db does not removed, db's that g does not added (live-ids: index scans, no entity read), and only the components those touch and their ancestors re-folded (reach-step), so a branch's graph is its base's at the cost of what it changed."], :schemas [[:form/keywords "10%"] [:seon.config.proofs/program-store "7%"] [:seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes "4%"] [:seon.program/inputs "4%"] [:seon.config.source/binary-vars "4%"]]}}
{:task "seon.source/a-tests-reach-is-the-same-however-the-program-was-analyzed", :plan "Done (root's ruling, 2026-10-25): a quoted name is data, by name (seon.program/quoted-names, the account's :quoted), never a reference, but in a macro's template, which is its expansion's code; the reach follows calls only; names-only and the respelled names (seon.reconcile/advance, seon.program's cited-by, seon.render.ui/did) are gone (docs/source.md). Left for the fork redesign: root's start as a var of its own, so the reach tells a forking test from one that starts a root. What it was: A test's class (process or not, seon.program/graph-index's :process?) and its digest depend on how the program db was built, not only on the program. Three observations. (1) Agent view: seon.source records a quoted name ((list 'seon.turn/work! ...) in seon.reconcile/advance) as a reach edge only when the named namespace was analyzed first, so 10 reconciler tests were process tests on one branch and not on main; hidden by building the symbol by name (4c7fd7800, the same trick as seon.render.ui/did). (2) Agent loop: calling seon.task/session-key twice in the reconciler's step moved 9 reconciler tests, seon.procs-test/reconciler and seon.problems-test/a-defect-in-the-reconciler-does-not-feed-itself into left out (241 -> 251); either call alone does not; reproduce from jolt/agent-loop's history, the commit before 0c2fa6434. (3) Measured on main d79bee61e (worktree reds-the-full-run-found): the same tree's plan (bin/test --plan both) on its kept program store, a branch refreshed from the nearest kept tree (test-runner/program-db, seon.source/refresh-tx), leaves out 240 (496 keys); on a fresh store (seon.source/import-tx of the whole tree) 250 (486 keys); the refreshed set is a strict subset (the 10 are 8 seon.reconcile-test, 1 seon.procs-test, 1 seon.problems-test). The branch's tree refreshed leaves out 188 (549 keys), fresh 251: refresh dropped 63 process edges. So keys and digests differ for one tree, which also breaks reuse by digest. Correct: the analysis gives the same facts and edges whatever the order and however many times a name appears, and an incremental refresh gives what a whole import gives (seon.program/reach-update's own rule: an update gives the digests a rebuild gives); the rule for a quoted name in a form (a call or not) is stated once (docs/references.md or the program page) and applied the same everywhere. Then the symbol-building tricks (seon.reconcile/advance, seon.render.ui/did) go. Proofs: one test's reach is equal under two analysis orders; the reach of one reconciler test is equal on both agent-loop versions; a tree's plan on a refreshed store equals its plan on a fresh store (keys, left out).", :changed #{"seon.docs/callers" "seon.render.ui/did" "seon.program/uses?" "seon.program/quoted-names" "seon.program/merge-accounts" "seon.program/unit-account" "seon.program/meaning-changes" ":form/quoted" "docs/source.md#write-back-mains-files-byte-for-byte" "seon.program/form-sets" ":form/refs" "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach" "seon.program/reach-step" "seon.program/account" ":seon.db/form-fact" "seon.program/macro-uses" "seon.reconcile/advance"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 66%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`). ```seon.program/reach-digests``` gives each test of a db its reach digest: its own form, its namespace's forms that define no var (the ns form, fixtures), and every live form their `:form/refs` reach (a var to the form that last defined it; a defined multimethod's methods and protocol's extensions), and every schema entry a reached var's contract names (and each entry the entry's form names, on), each form and entry digested once; plus the checkout's inputs with no program facts (`seon.fx/inputs`: deps.edn, bin/, resources/ with the schemas contracts name, test fixtures and every submodule pin, Jolt's included; not task files or prose; and the per-test tick bound), plus the values of the config keys its reach reads (```seon.program/config-reads```: the `seon.config.*` keys its reached forms name, every key for one referencing the config map from outside `seon.config`, such as root's boot; their values from the config in force, ```seon.fx/config-input```: the shipped file, the `--config` layers the command line names, the `--set` overrides and the `#env` values under the profile, with the checkout's path normalized and the place keys left out), plus the vars data names that root resolves by name (`seon.fx/declared-refs`: system.edn's procs, edges of `seon.system/read-decl`), and no tree: a test whose reached forms call a file reader or run the binary (`seon.program/tree-readers`; a test runs target/seon, bin/root or bin/check only through `seon.test-store/binary-sh`) is digested by what it reaches like any other (owner, 2026-10-27: \"no tests should include all of src\"; with the tree it was 286 tests rerun on any edit), and a file or a binary change outside its reach is found by the full run (`bin/test --all`, below). A check step whose reach reads the tree still takes it. A test's digest moves exactly when a form or schema entry it reaches does. A literal name a form passes to `resolve`, `ns-resolve` or `requiring-resolve` is a reference (`seon.program/resolved`): the var it looks up at run time is one it reaches, so a test that resolves its var by name is no green reused after that var changes, or once it comes to exist. Any other name a form holds as data (quoted: `tree-readers`, `env-readers`, `seon.config/schema-forms`, whose `:seon.render/*` properties name every render, a form sent to an agent) is no reference, so reaching that form is neither using nor reaching what it names (below)." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 26%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check. The merge's proof is `bin/prove` on the merged tree, then `bin/check smoke`; `bin/laws` (every law's sites) and `health` ([health](health.md)) run inside it as steps keyed by the whole tree. One rule selects for everyone: a key runs exactly when no green result is stored at its reach digest (```seon.proofs/plan```), in the one results directory every checkout shares (`:seon.config.source/test-results-dir`), so an unchanged tree runs nothing and a result proven in any checkout is reused in every other." "docs/source.md#mains-program-follows-the-tree 20%: Main's program is the tree's: at each boot, when the files read differently from main's last import (their digest differs from the fact's `:digest`, or none is recorded), main's program is replaced by the tree's in one transaction, ```seon.source/replace-tx```; when they read the same, nothing is imported or written. So a resumed main follows its tree when worktree sessions merge and the tree moves on in git, or the next generation's tree holds Seon's own merges commit. Re-importing changed files alone would not do: a form's identity is its text (`seon.program/account-tx`), so a changed file's new forms would join its old ones, and a removed function would stay live. `replace-tx` is ```seon.source/import-tx```, read against main without the forms of the imported namespaces (each form takes its file's order and lead), with every program entity of those namespaces and of the schema resources that the import no longer makes retracted: a changed function's old form, a removed function's form and var, a removed file's namespace. Data (tasks, runs, turns, config and root facts) and the program of a namespace no file holds (an example program, a namespace an agent made) are untouched; history keeps the old program. A form or schema entry main holds already keeps its `:form/source` as it is, so a form merged in (which the binary does not hold) is still loaded at boot, and a form new to main is the binary's. The digest is of what was read (the files and the reference-code pins), not of the commit alone, so uncommitted edits in the tree count, and a merges commit made by `update-ref` under a checked-out tree (its files unchanged) imports nothing." "docs/source.md#shared-build-caches 13%: `bin/env` points Jolt's three compile caches at ``target/cache/`` of the main tree, found from git's common dir, so every worktree and generation (`bin/worktree --at`) shares them: `build` (`JOLT_BUILD_CACHE_DIR`, build units), `runtime` (`JOLT_RUNTIME_CACHE_DIR`) and `aot` (`JOLT_CACHE_DIR`, one artifact per namespace, keyed by its source, its requires' keys and what its compile read)." "docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt 12%: ``seon.ctx/build`` walks the pattern once, when the agent's state first holds its db (a spawn, a resume, a clone), and ``seon.ctx/patch`` keeps it from each transaction's datoms, which ``seon.agent/transact!`` records with the db they made."], :functions [["seon.root/import-source! 5%: Main's program follows Seon's own source (seon.source): root's dirs read through the :source effect (first brought to the main checkout's branch, seon.fx/read-source: fast-forwarded when behind, a recorded gap when it holds commits main lacks; their files, the tree's HEAD commit and its reference-code pins, and the tree's inputs with no program facts, seon.fx/inputs)." [:form/id :ns/file :schema/file :seon.error/message :seon.error/subject :seon.root/id :seon.root/source]] ["seon.reconcile/start 4%: Begin action act's flight." [:seon.reconcile/action :seon.reconcile/state :seon.run/agent :seon.run/end-ms :seon.run/outcome :seon.run/reason :seon.run/status]] "seon.program/unit-account 4%: What the compiler says one analyzed top-level node does, from its IR by resolved var identity: the vars it defines at its top (:defs, each def-info) and inside a function body (:inline-defs), its multimethod methods ({:multi sym-as-written :dispatch value}) and protocol extensions ({:protocol sym :type name}), the multimethods it sets up, the vars and host statics (Class/member) it references (:refs, with each var a literal name passed to resolve, ns-resolve or requiring-resolve looks up; in a macro, also the names its template quotes, which its expansion calls), the qualified names its quoted data holds (:quoted: quoted-names, data, not references), the qualified keywords its constants name (:keywords: the attributes, config keys and schema entries its code reads or writes), the banned vars it references, the calls it bounds by a clock (:timeouts: deref with a timeout), its catch clauses (:catches, each {:class :answers}), the namespaces it requires, and whether it changes its namespace's environment (:env?)." ["seon.agent/test-plan 4%: What judges branch db, as this process holds it: seon.proofs/plan of its tests' reach digests (seon.program/reach-digests) against base's (main at its merge base), the greens stored at those digests (files in results-dir, and the test service's result facts in db, greens and reds: seon.proofs/stored-results) and proofs." [:form/src :ns/form :ns/name :seon.config.source/test-results-dir :var/form :var/kind :var/name]] "seon.contract/checked 4%: f checked by contract form c (compiled once, here: seon.contract/wrap), errors named fn-sym; f itself when instrumentation is off."], :schemas [[:seon.config.source/process-vars "7%"] [:seon.config.source/binary-vars "4%"] [:seon.task/state "4%"] [:seon.problems/class "3%"] [:seon.reconcile/step "2%"]]}}
{:task "seon.context/related-weighs-a-neighbour-by-its-rarity", :plan "```seon.context/related``` ranks the call graph's functions by how many of the plan's cited vars reach each (by-frequency over their callers and callees), so what every contracted form names comes first: on a worktree root (2026-10-25, the browser check of seon.context/the-screen-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan) its five functions were seon.contract/checked, seon.contract/on?, seon.agent/db, seon.contract/error? and seon.db/eid, the plumbing each `defn` expands to or calls, none of what the task changes. The comparison that set the default (docs/vector.md#what-the-work-is-likely-to-need-as-an-eval: functions 0.50 at 5 fused) counted the same callees, so this is the measured default's noise, not a regression. The field's way: weigh a neighbour by its inverse document frequency (log of the forms over the forms naming it: one AVET count of `:form/refs` a candidate), so a callee every form names weighs nothing. Measure first: rerun notes/related-search/search.clj's graph ranking with IDF weights on the same 32 merged tasks; keep it only if functions R@5 rises. No new attribute: the counts are seeks on the index there is. Measured 2026-10-25 (worktree related-weighs-a-neighbour-by-its-rarity, on its root with every form, section, schema and plan embedded; notes/related-search/search3.clj and search3b.clj over search.clj, results in results3.edn and results3b.edn): the 24 merged tasks whose merge changed functions their plan does not cite, what the plan cites left out as related does. The graph alone at 5: 0.099 by count, 0.214 with IDF (8 tasks better, none worse). Fused with meaning, what the screen shows: R@5 0.343 to 0.353 (3 better, 1 worse, 20 the same), R@10 0.401 to 0.423. Of the fused top 5 over 31 tasks (155 lines), plumbing (seon.contract/checked, on?, error?, seon.config/value, seon.agent/db, seon.db/eid) filled 26 by count and 1 with IDF: the lines change, the recall barely does, because what replaces them is rarely what the merge changed either. Built (root, 2026-10-25: \"don't render crap\": 26 of 155 screen lines were plumbing): a neighbour weighs 1/(1 + the forms naming it), counted by bounded-count to :seon.config.render/related-rarity (16), which needs no count of every form (log IDF would) and reads at most 16 datoms a neighbour, so the graph half costs O(neighbours x 16) more. Measured at caps 16, 64 and 256 alike (notes/related-search/search3c.clj): fused R@5 0.346 against 0.343 by counts (log IDF 0.353), plumbing lines 26 to 0 of 155. On two live tasks' screens (seon.context/a-first-turns-context-costs-what-it-shows, seon.problems/a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows) seon.contract/checked and on? gave way to seon.ref/kept, seon.task/citation-problems, seon.ref/cited and seon.render.ai/problems, seon.check/health-check: the lines changed, not their number. The :macro clause stays (a macro reached by meaning is weighed by nothing).", :changed #{"seon.context/related" "docs/vector.md#fused-with-the-call-graph" ":seon.config.render/related-rarity"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/vector.md#fused-with-the-call-graph 79%: The task's data shows `:similar` (```seon.context/similar```): the first `:seon.config.render/similar` program functions nearest the plan's vector, by an exact search (`near` with every vector its shortlist, at every dim), that the plan and proofs do not name, a line each. On Seon's own program (3,843 vectors) it costs about 9.7M ticks and 52 MB (40 ms) and 397 of a task's data's 1,749 tokens; ```seon.vector-cost-test/similar-ranks-every-form-once``` holds it. Step 6 measures how often agents read one; 0 turns it off. Fusing the call graph's helpers (the cited vars' and proofs' one-hop callees, tf-idf) with the forms nearest a task's plan by reciprocal rank (```seon.vector/rrf```, k `:seon.config.vector/rrf-k`) was measured below; embeddings alone did as well, so the task's data uses them alone, and the fusion stays for further exploration." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 20%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)." "docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt 5%: ``seon.ctx/build`` walks the pattern once, when the agent's state first holds its db (a spawn, a resume, a clone), and ``seon.ctx/patch`` keeps it from each transaction's datoms, which ``seon.agent/transact!`` records with the db they made." "docs/health.md#a-dead-citation 4%: A wiki section stores the names its own text cites as references (`:seon.section/names`, written by `seon.docs/load-tx` through `seon.ref/kept`)." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 2%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check."], :functions [["my.task/related 21%: What this task's plan, as the branch holds it now, is likely to need beyond what it cites: the\n  wiki sections, program functions and schema keys nearest it by its references and by meaning,\n  :seon.config.render/related of each (seon.context/related, a render over the plan's vectors),\n  each a line at summary depth with the forms that read one whole and search further\n  (seon.context/related-view). (my.agent/related \"words\" k) refines it by any text." [:seon.task/id]] ["seon.context/related-view 13%: What `related` answers, each a line at summary depth: a section its address and its lead's first sentence (seon.docs/view; its heading when it has no lead), a function its name and its doc's first sentence, beside the registry keys it operates on when it names any ([line keys], its :form/keywords), a schema key itself; with the forms that read one whole (naming the text's key: a step-6 run's model read the view with (:text ...) eight times over) and search further: what my.task/related and my.agent/related answer." [:form/keywords :schema/key :var/doc :var/form :var/name]] ["seon.render.ui/related 13%: What the task's plan is likely to need beyond what it cites (a component over ctx and the db): seon.context/related over the db as seon.context/related-view lines it (each section its first sentence, each function its doc's, with the forms that read more), what (my.task/related) answers, as a call of the one printer; a line saying so when there is nothing yet." [:seon.config.render/value-budget :seon.ctx/ctx :seon.render/opts]] ["seon.vector/rrf 10%: Rankings fused by reciprocal rank (Cormack et al." [:seon.config.vector/rrf-k]] ["seon.agent/test-plan 10%: What judges branch db, as this process holds it: seon.proofs/plan of its tests' reach digests (seon.program/reach-digests) against base's (main at its merge base), the greens stored at those digests (files in results-dir, and the test service's result facts in db, greens and reds: seon.proofs/stored-results) and proofs." [:form/src :ns/form :ns/name :seon.config.source/test-results-dir :var/form :var/kind :var/name]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.render/related-rarity "24%"] [:seon.config.render/related-seeds-near "15%"] [:form/refs "7%"] [:seon.config.render/related "6%"] [:seon.problems/class "5%"]]}}
{:task "seon.vector/a-text-written-on-a-branch-is-embedded-there", :plan "What exists (docs/vector.md#what-is-embedded): forms, sections and task plans are embedded on main by `seon.vector/vectors-proc` at the writer's commit notice; an agent's branch reads main's vectors as of its fork, so a plan the agent rewrites with `my.task/update!`, a function it defines, or a section text it changes has no vector until it merges. So `my.agent/similar` on its own new function answers [], and anything ranked by the plan's vector (the task data's :similar, the related sections of `seon.context/the-screen-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan`) cannot follow the plan as the agent refines it over its run. An agent already reaches the model: `my.agent/similar` asks root's :embed effect over its socket (`agent/fx! :embed`), and the answer is plain data. The change: at the end of each turn's evals, the agent computes ```seon.vector/due``` on its branch (texts whose vector is missing or made from an earlier text: the plan changed, a new form) restricted to what the branch wrote since its fork, asks :embed once for all of them (batched as the effect already batches), and transacts ```seon.vector/tx-data``` on its branch; a failed embedding is the effect's recorded error and the texts stay due. Main is unchanged: a branch's vectors merge with its entities (check that the merge carries `:seon.vector/*` facts, or that main re-embeds the merged texts, whichever the merge already does; say which). Decided (2026-10-25, this worktree): the merge carries no :seon.vector/* facts (platform records: `seon.merge/carried-tx` carries program keys and the plan and instructions only), so main's `seon.vector/vectors-proc` embeds the merged texts again at the merge commit's notice, as any text new on main; carrying them would add the vector attributes to the carried set for one ~10 ms embedding per text saved. The candidates are ```seon.vector/written``` (a datoms-diff from the db the last call read, at first the fork's base: the cost of what changed, not of the store) plus the task's own plan (main may not have embedded a plan made just before the fork), judged by ```seon.vector/due``` over those datoms only (one seek each); ```seon.agent/embed-written!``` runs at the start of each turn's observe, before the screen is made, and takes its count from :seon.config.vector/texts (0, bin/test's roots: asks nothing). No new attribute, no thread in the agent (the model stays in root). Cost proof: a turn that writes nothing due asks nothing.", :changed #{"seon.context/related-at" "seon.vector/texts" "seon.context/related-view" "seon.context/related" "docs/vector.md#what-is-embedded" "seon.screen/forms" "my.task/related" "seon.program/quoted-var?" "seon.repl/response" "seon.vector/written" "seon.vector/due" "seon.turn/observe" "seon.context/by-frequency" "seon.program/unit-account" "seon.program/schema-dependents" "seon.agent/embed-written!" "seon.agent/fx!" "docs/decisions.md#related-things-are-found-by-measured-search-over-every-kind-through-stored-edges" "seon.program/meaning" "docs/render.md#cuts-in-a-display-namespace-that-are-not-cuts-of-output" "seon.program/quoted-vars" "my.agent/similar" "docs/vector.md#what-the-work-is-likely-to-need-as-an-eval" "docs/vector.md#vectors" "my.agent/query-of" "seon.source/defining-meanings" "seon.context/related-count" "seon.vector/due-among" "seon.context/turn-state" "my.agent/related" "seon.vector/rrf" ":seon.config.render/related"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/vector.md#what-the-work-is-likely-to-need-as-an-eval 22%: The page's `[:related]` part (```seon.render.ui/related```), which the model reads with the rest of the page, and `(my.task/related)` at the REPL, show what the plan's work is likely to need beyond what the plan cites (```seon.context/related```):" "docs/vector.md#the-pyramid 11%: A Matryoshka vector's prefix is itself an embedding, so one stored vector serves two levels (`native/vector.c`, in libprobe: no threads, every process loads it)." "docs/vector.md#fused-with-the-call-graph 10%: The task's data shows `:similar` (```seon.context/similar```): the first `:seon.config.render/similar` program functions nearest the plan's vector, by an exact search (`near` with every vector its shortlist, at every dim), that the plan and proofs do not name, a line each." "docs/parity.md#the-agents-repl-my 10%: Each v1 function of the agent's REPL and where it stands here." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 8%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data."], :functions ["seon.vector/round 8%: At main's db, the next round: the first :texts due (:seon.config.vector/texts; 0 asks nothing) go to the :embed effect." ["seon.vector/texts 6%: Each embedded kind: the attribute holding its text, and the text the model reads." [:form/src :schema/form :seon.section/text :seon.task/plan]] ["seon.turn/observe 5%: What a turn starts from, measured now: the proofs run by the test service (seon.prove: one run per branch and tx whoever asks, in a fork of this agent; a run it could not fork ran no proof: every proof is unknown, and the refused answer is the run's error), the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (seon.merge/uncontracted, as root's judge and the merge gate ask it), its unmerged entries, the problems its work made true (seon.problems/caused, as root's judge asks it), the done rule over them, and the task as the branch holds it (its plan may have improved): the done rule's inputs, nothing else." [:seon.task/id :seon.task/kind :seon.task/plan]] "seon.program/attr-namespaces 4%: The namespaces of the attributes that hold the program (its forms, their definitions, the schema and the db's own schema): what a merge carries as program, and what a commit must touch to move the reach graph (seon.fork-server's :fork)." ["seon.contract/on? 4%: Are the contracts on in this process (:seon.config.contract/instrument)? :off: no wrapper and no recording; :contracts: wrappers, no recording transaction (for measuring the two apart); :on: both." [:seon.config.contract/instrument]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.vector/texts "40%"] [:seon.config.source/tree-excludes "5%"] [:seon.task/state "4%"] [:seon.problems/class "4%"] [:seon.config.source/gate-checkout "2%"]]}}
{:task "seon.server/done-answers-the-verdict", :plan "Dogfood (gap 4): `seon_done` answers `{:judging ...}` and ends the agent, so an outside agent never sees whether its run was done or what the done rule refused. The judge's reasons are recorded on the run. The change: the op answers when the judge has, with its outcome and reasons (as the screen's :refused shows them), the request's one answer (a reply with credit, never a wait on a clock).", :changed #{"seon.root/defect" "seon.server/ops" "docs/server.md#ops" "seon.server/verdict" "seon.root/await-db"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/server.md#ops 49%: The ops are one table, ```seon.server/ops```, keyed by a request's `:op`; each entry holds its description, its arguments as JSON Schema and its answer function, and nothing else lists them: a new op is a new MCP tool `seon_<op>` (`seon_agents`, `seon_eval`, `seon_context`, `seon_message`, `seon_attach`, `seon_done`, `seon_prove`), and `bin/check mcp` compares the tools the server lists with the table, never a count." "docs/tasks.md#the-done-rule 18%: `seon.reconcile/done-explain` says why a run's measured facts do not make its task done; empty means done." "docs/root.md#runs-and-judging 16%: A run is one agent's try at a task; the reconciler starts it, and judges its end from measured facts only." "docs/server.md#the-server 10%: Root's server is one socket on 127.0.0.1 where outside agents (a Claude session through MCP, a person with `nc`) send requests, one per line, and read one answer per line: `seon.server`." "docs/turn.md#one-run-from-release-to-judge 8%: Root's reconciler forks an agent for a released task and evaluates ```seon.turn/work!``` in it, naming the task, the run and `:seon.config.agent/max-turns`."], :functions [["seon.server/ops 11%: The server's ops, the one table, keyed by a request's :op (an EDN request is a keyword-keyed map, {:op :agents}; a line opening `{\"` is MCP's, respond): each op's description, its arguments (JSON Schema properties; :required, the ones it needs) and its answer, a fn of root's system, the agent the request names (:root, an agent's keyword, or nil) and the request." [:seon.error/message :seon.error/type :seon.run/agent :seon.run/id :seon.run/status :seon.run/task]] ["seon.turn/hand-back! 9%: End run of task as an outside agent hands it back (seon.server's :done): the proofs run again, and the run's end written as the turn loop writes its own (end!: the live runtime staged, the end committed on the branch), :green when the done rule has no reason, else :red with the reasons." [:seon.run/id :seon.task/id :seon.turn/end]] ["seon.root/await-db 5%: Main's first db value (now or after a later commit) for which (pred db) holds: a watch on main's connection runs pred once per commit." [:seon.root/no-agent :seon.root/quiet-before-true]] ["seon.reconcile/start 5%: Begin action act's flight." [:seon.reconcile/action :seon.reconcile/state :seon.run/agent :seon.run/end-ms :seon.run/outcome :seon.run/reason :seon.run/status]] ["seon.turn/observe 5%: What a turn starts from, measured now: the proofs run by the test service (seon.prove: one run per branch and tx whoever asks, in a fork of this agent; a run it could not fork ran no proof: every proof is unknown, and the refused answer is the run's error), the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (seon.merge/uncontracted, as root's judge and the merge gate ask it), its unmerged entries, the problems its work made true (seon.problems/caused, as root's judge asks it), the done rule over them, and the task as the branch holds it (its plan may have improved): the done rule's inputs, nothing else." [:seon.task/id :seon.task/kind :seon.task/plan]]], :schemas [[:seon.turn/end "10%"] [:seon.task/state "8%"] [:seon.reconcile/not-done "7%"] [:seon.run/status "5%"] [:seon.config.agent/form-ticks "5%"]]}}
{:task "seon.reconcile/the-judge-asks-the-test-service", :plan "Left over from task seon.proofs/one-prove-path. The judge's `:tested` step (`seon.reconcile/advance`) asks the agent's `:tests` op directly ({:op :tests :id agent}), beside the test service every turn uses (`seon.agent/prove!` -> `seon.prove`). So a judge's run is not recorded on main (no `seon.prove/result-tx` facts, no `:seon.proof.run`), is not joined with a session's or the turn's request for the same key, and the outside agents' `:judge` input repeats it. Correct: the judge asks `seon.prove` for the branch at the run's end with mode :judge, keyed by `seon.prove/read-digest` of the branch's program as the turn's is; its answer is the run's results; one prove path (the audit's P4: drop the reconciler's direct `:tests`, `seon.root/run-tests!`'s use by the judge). The reconciler does not hold the branch's program digest today: the agent computes it, so either the judge evaluates `(seon.agent/prove! {:mode :judge})` in the agent (one eval, as `:worked` evaluates work!) or the digest becomes a run fact the agent writes at its end. Touches the reconciler's judge flight: after the agent-loop parts merge (they change which ends are judged). Proof: a judge after a turn at the same digest is answered by the service from the turn's run (one `:seon.proof.run` fact for both), and its results are on main. As built (2026-10-25): an eval's reply to root carries no value (`seon.agent/handle!` drops it), so the judge sends the agent a `:judge` request and the agent calls `(seon.agent/prove! :judge)`; the service then sends it `:tests` as for any branch an agent holds. The judge's key is mode :judge, the turn's :turn (they select different tests), so they are two runs; the judge reruns nothing the turn left, since every result at a digest is reused (task seon.proofs/a-red-at-its-digest-is-reused).", :changed #{"docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches" "seon.root/ext-op" "docs/merging.md#the-test-service" "seon.agent/handle!" "seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc" "seon.reconcile/advance" "seon.agent/prove!"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/merging.md#the-test-service 47%: One proc, `:prove` (```seon.prove/prove-proc```, declared in `resources/seon/system.edn`), admits every request to run tests, whoever asks: a Claude session (the server's `:prove` op, the MCP tool `seon_prove`, ```seon.root/prove!```) and an agent's turn (```seon.agent/prove!```, which ```seon.turn/observe``` calls) share it, so a change is proved once." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 18%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check." "docs/turn.md#observe 16%: Each turn starts from facts measured now, never remembered: `seon.turn/observe` asks the test service (`seon.agent/prove!`, `seon.prove`: one run per branch and tx whoever asks, in a fork of the agent) to run the task's accumulated proofs and the tests the branch changed, each test bounded by `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` (a run that cannot be made ran no proof, so every proof is unknown and the run's error is `:test-run`), the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (`seon.merge/uncontracted`, the question root's judge asks), its unmerged entries, the problems its own work made true (`seon.problems/caused`: it refuses done), and the done rule over them (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`: [tasks](tasks.md#the-done-rule)), read from the reconciler's index of the branch, which the agent holds across turns and observes from the last turn's db over what differs (`seon.turn/index!`, `seon.reconcile/index`): listing it again every turn cost 92M ticks and 384 MB on a main of 680 tasks, observing it 1.3M and 10 MB." "docs/plan.md#the-plan-1 8%: The destination is **E: a request worked as one whole task by a Seon agent, end to end, measured as turns to green.** In order:" "docs/server.md#ops 6%: The ops are one table, ```seon.server/ops```, keyed by a request's `:op`; each entry holds its description, its arguments as JSON Schema and its answer function, and nothing else lists them: a new op is a new MCP tool `seon_<op>` (`seon_agents`, `seon_eval`, `seon_context`, `seon_message`, `seon_attach`, `seon_done`, `seon_prove`), and `bin/check mcp` compares the tools the server lists with the table, never a count."], :functions [["seon.agent/run-tests-forked 6%: Fork this agent; the child runs what test-plan runs for proofs (the selected tests with no green held at their digest), each test within ticks engine ticks of work of its own, writing each test var as it starts and then one EDN line of its result on a pipe." [:seon.error/message :seon.test/fork-refused :seon.test/result]] ["seon.root/prove! 5%: Ask the test service (seon.prove) to prove a branch at its head, the tests of namespaces names (a set of symbols; none: all it selects): agent id's own branch in mode :turn (as its turn proves it; mode :judge, as root's judge does), or main (agent nil) in mode :main (every test with no result at its digest: seon.agent/run-tests-forked)." [:seon.agent/id :seon.root/sys]] "my.agent/prove 5%: Run what root's judge will run on this branch, through the test service (seon.agent/prove! :judge, so the run is recorded on main and is one run with any other asker's of this head): the task's proofs and every in-process test reaching what the branch changed since the main it forked from, each test with a result at its reach digest reused (a red as recorded), not run; given proofs, the tests of their namespaces." ["seon.turn/observe 5%: What a turn starts from, measured now: the proofs run by the test service (seon.prove: one run per branch and tx whoever asks, in a fork of this agent; a run it could not fork ran no proof: every proof is unknown, and the refused answer is the run's error), the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (seon.merge/uncontracted, as root's judge and the merge gate ask it), its unmerged entries, the problems its work made true (seon.problems/caused, as root's judge asks it), the done rule over them, and the task as the branch holds it (its plan may have improved): the done rule's inputs, nothing else." [:seon.task/id :seon.task/kind :seon.task/plan]] ["seon.contract/on? 4%: Are the contracts on in this process (:seon.config.contract/instrument)? :off: no wrapper and no recording; :contracts: wrappers, no recording transaction (for measuring the two apart); :on: both." [:seon.config.contract/instrument]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.agent/test-ticks "15%"] [:seon.task/state "6%"] [:seon.reconcile/step "4%"] [:seon.config.proofs/jobs "3%"] [:seon.config.agent/stall-ms "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.proofs/a-red-at-its-digest-is-reused", :plan "Left over from task seon.proofs/one-prove-path. Only greens are stored and reused by digest (`seon.proofs/store-greens!`, `seon.proofs/greens`, `seon.proofs/stored-greens`), so a red reruns at every ask: the judge after a red turn reruns every red the turn found, and so does each next turn on an unchanged tree. Since one-prove-path a test's verdict is a function of the test and its digest alone (one per-test tick bound everywhere), so a red at a digest is as reusable as a green (AGENTS section 5: a test's result is keyed by the digest of everything it reaches; a flaky red is a defect to fix at its cause, never a reason to rerun). Correct: the test service's red facts on main (`seon.prove/result-tx`: `:seon.proof/status` and `:seon.proof/failures` at `:seon.proof/digest`) are read by `seon.agent/test-plan` like greens, and a red reused is reported as red with its recorded failures, marked `:reused`. What reads 'a red runs again' today must change with it: proofs_test's shards-split-by-namespace-and-each-result-is-stored asserts it. Perf: a red turn's judge forks nothing for its reds. Question for root: reuse reds only from the test service's facts (recorded with failures), not from the results directory (which stores greens only).", :changed #{"seon.agent/run-tests-forked" "docs/merging.md#the-test-service" "seon.agent/test-plan" "seon.proofs/plan" "my.agent/prove" "seon.proofs/stored-results" "seon.prove/result-tx"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/merging.md#the-test-service 58%: One proc, `:prove` (```seon.prove/prove-proc```, declared in `resources/seon/system.edn`), admits every request to run tests, whoever asks: a Claude session (the server's `:prove` op, the MCP tool `seon_prove`, ```seon.root/prove!```) and an agent's turn (```seon.agent/prove!```, which ```seon.turn/observe``` calls) share it, so a change is proved once." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 30%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check. The merge's proof is `bin/prove` on the merged tree, then `bin/check smoke`; `bin/laws` (every law's sites) and `health` ([health](health.md)) run inside it as steps keyed by the whole tree. One rule selects for everyone: a key runs exactly when no green result is stored at its reach digest (```seon.proofs/plan```), in the one results directory every checkout shares (`:seon.config.source/test-results-dir`), so an unchanged tree runs nothing and a result proven in any checkout is reused in every other." "docs/health.md#a-flaky-test 23%: A test's verdict is a function of its reach digest, so one that judged both green and red at one digest is a defect at its cause (a clock, a race, an input its digest misses), never a reason to run it again. Every verdict is kept: the results directory holds `<digest>.edn` for a green and `<digest>.red.edn` for a red, and the test service's facts keep every status a digest had in their history. `seon.proofs/verdict` judges a digest that holds both as `seon.proofs/flaky`: red, its failures led by one saying so, never reused as green and never run again until a change moves the digest. `bin/test` and `bin/prove` print it (`FLAKY test digest`) and end red, an agent's plan judges it red, and `seon.problems/test-reds` shows it as class `:flaky` (area tests) once the test service records it. Fixed at the cause: the test, or what it reaches, changes, and the new digest judges again. Not kept: a test that never reported (its namespace's run threw first), which has no verdict of its own, and a `bin/check` step's red (a step missing from bin/check's greens may have been cut short, not judged), so a step is never flaky." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 16%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)." "docs/parity.md#tasks-merges-tests 12%: Each v1 capability for tasks, merges and tests, and where it stands here."], :functions [["seon.proofs/stored-results 23%: {digest result} of the results db holds at digests (:seon.proof/ facts, which the test service\n  records, seon.prove/result-tx): one AVET seek per digest, and one seek of its status's history\n  (Datahike's, :seon.config.store/keep-history?): every verdict the digest ever had. A green is\n  {:digest :pass 1 :fail 0 :error 0}, the shape verdicts reads from the files; a red is its status\n  counted once, with the failures it recorded (:failures [{:type :message :expected :actual}]); a\n  digest that was both is flaky (a store with :seon.config.store/keep-history? off keeps one\n  status, so it finds none). A test's verdict is a function of its digest (one per-test tick\n  bound everywhere), so a red is reused as a green is." [:seon.proof/digest :seon.proof/failures :seon.proof/status]] ["seon.problems/test-reds 7%: Each test whose newest result on main (the test service's, seon.prove/result-tx: the entity per test under :seon.proof/latest) is red, as a :test-red row naming the test, the branch and tx it ran at and the change that asked." [:seon.problems/row :seon.proof/branch :seon.proof/change :seon.proof/failures :seon.proof/latest :seon.proof/run :seon.proof/status :seon.proof/t]] ["seon.agent/run-tests-forked 6%: Fork this agent; the child runs what test-plan runs for proofs (the selected tests with no green held at their digest), each test within ticks engine ticks of work of its own, writing each test var as it starts and then one EDN line of its result on a pipe." [:seon.error/message :seon.test/fork-refused :seon.test/result]] "seon.proofs/plan 5%: What a change runs: of cand ({key digest}, the candidate's), base (the base's {key digest}, nil: none), found ({digest result}, the results stored at cand's digests: found over verdicts and stored-results) and proofs (keys that always judge), {:selected :run :reused}." "seon.proofs/verdicts 5%: {digest result} of what dir holds at digests: a green (<digest>.edn), or flaky when the digest also holds a red (<digest>.red.edn)."], :schemas [[:seon.config.source/test-results-dir "12%"] [:seon.proof/status "9%"] [:seon.proof/digest "6%"] [:seon.config.proofs/max-assertions "4%"] [:seon.task/state "4%"]]}}
